window.FERN_DATA = window.FERN_DATA || {};
FERN_DATA["python"] = {"units": [{"id": "py-w1", "title": "Week 1 · Setup and first look", "blurb": "Get Python running on your Mac and see how it differs from Java. ~4 hrs.", "topics": [{"id": "py-w1-plan", "title": "This week's plan", "src": "Course plan", "idea": "Six short sessions: install, the REPL, your first script, and a side-by-side with Java.", "deep": "<p>About <strong>4 hours</strong> this week, in 6 sessions. Do one per sitting.</p><ol><li><strong>30 min:</strong> Install Python 3.13 with Homebrew and check the version</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> Play in the REPL (tutorial ch. 2–3.1)</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> Make a project folder and a venv, write hello.py</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> Python vs Java: read the comparison card, rewrite one mooc.fi exercise</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> learnpython.org: Hello World + Variables drills</li><li><strong>30 min:</strong> Exercise + explain-it-back question</li></ol><p>Reading: <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/interpreter.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tutorial 2. Using the interpreter</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/introduction.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">3. An informal introduction</a>.</p>", "text": "about 4 hours this week, in 6 sessions. do one per sitting. 30 min: install python 3.13 with homebrew and check the version 35 min: play in the repl (tutorial ch. 2–3.1) 40 min: make a project folder and a venv, write hello.py 40 min: python vs java: read the comparison card, rewrite one mooc.fi exercise 35 min: learnpython.org: hello world + variables drills 30 min: exercise + explain-it-back question reading: tutorial 2. using the interpreter , 3. an informal introduction ."}, {"id": "py-w1-install", "title": "Install Python on your Mac", "src": "Python docs", "idea": "macOS doesn't ship a current Python for you to use. Install one with Homebrew, then always type python3, never python.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code>brew<span class=\"w\"> </span>install<span class=\"w\"> </span>python@3.13\npython3<span class=\"w\"> </span>--version<span class=\"w\">     </span><span class=\"c1\"># Python 3.13.x</span>\nwhich<span class=\"w\"> </span>python3<span class=\"w\">         </span><span class=\"c1\"># /opt/homebrew/bin/python3 on an M4 Mac</span></code></pre></div><ul><li><code>python3</code> starts the REPL. Leave it with <code>exit()</code> or Ctrl+D.</li><li><code>pip3</code> installs packages. You'll use it inside a venv from week 7 on.</li></ul><p>Same idea as checking <code>java --version</code> after installing a JDK. <span class=\"by\">Added by Fern</span></p>", "text": "brew install python@3.13 python3 --version # python 3.13.x which python3 # /opt/homebrew/bin/python3 on an m4 mac python3 starts the repl. leave it with exit() or ctrl+d. pip3 installs packages. you'll use it inside a venv from week 7 on. same idea as checking java --version after installing a jdk. added by fern"}, {"id": "py-w1-repl", "title": "The REPL: Python's scratchpad", "src": "Tutorial ch. 2", "idea": "Type a line, press Enter, see the result right away. It's like JShell, but you'll use it far more.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"o\">&gt;&gt;&gt;</span> <span class=\"mi\">2</span> <span class=\"o\">**</span> <span class=\"mi\">10</span>\n<span class=\"mi\">1024</span>\n<span class=\"o\">&gt;&gt;&gt;</span> <span class=\"n\">name</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"Noah\"</span>\n<span class=\"o\">&gt;&gt;&gt;</span> <span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Hi, </span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">!\"</span>\n<span class=\"s1\">'Hi, Noah!'</span>\n<span class=\"o\">&gt;&gt;&gt;</span> <span class=\"n\">_</span> <span class=\"o\">*</span> <span class=\"mi\">2</span>          <span class=\"c1\"># _ is the last result</span>\n<span class=\"s1\">'Hi, Noah!Hi, Noah!'</span></code></pre></div><ul><li><code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> is the prompt. <code>...</code> means Python is waiting for the rest of a block.</li><li><code>help(str)</code> and <code>dir(str)</code> show what a type can do, with no browser needed.</li></ul>", "text": ">>> 2 ** 10 1024 >>> name = \"noah\" >>> f \"hi, { name } !\" 'hi, noah!' >>> _ * 2 # _ is the last result 'hi, noah!hi, noah!' >>> is the prompt. ... means python is waiting for the rest of a block. help(str) and dir(str) show what a type can do, with no browser needed."}, {"id": "py-w1-vs-java", "title": "Python vs Java at a glance", "src": "Fern comparison", "idea": "No class or main method needed, no semicolons, no type declarations, and indentation is the syntax instead of braces.", "deep": "<p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Python</strong></p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"c1\"># hello.py</span>\n<span class=\"n\">name</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"nb\">input</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Name? \"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Hello, </span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">!\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">else</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Hello, stranger!\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span></code></pre></div><p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Java</strong> (what you already know)</p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kd\">public</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kd\">class</span> <span class=\"nc\">Hello</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"kd\">public</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kd\">static</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">void</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">main</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"o\">[]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">args</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"n\">Scanner</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">sc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">new</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Scanner</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">System</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">in</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"n\">System</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">out</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"Name? \"</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">sc</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">nextLine</span><span class=\"p\">();</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"k\">if</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"o\">!</span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">isEmpty</span><span class=\"p\">())</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">            </span><span class=\"n\">System</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">out</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">println</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"Hello, \"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s\">\"!\"</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"p\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">else</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">            </span><span class=\"n\">System</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">out</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">println</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"Hello, stranger!\"</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span></code></pre></div><ul><li><strong>Typing:</strong> Java checks types when it compiles. Python checks them while it runs, so a type mistake shows up as a crash at that line.</li><li><strong>Blocks:</strong> the colon plus 4 spaces replace <code>{ }</code>. Mixing tabs and spaces is an error.</li><li><strong>Running:</strong> no <code>javac</code> step. <code>python3 hello.py</code> runs the file.</li><li><strong>Empty string is falsy</strong>, so <code>if name:</code> works like <code>!name.isEmpty()</code>.</li></ul><details><summary>🔁 Explain it back: why does Python crash at runtime for a type mistake that Java would catch earlier?</summary><p>Point at the word that changes: <em>when</em> the types get checked. Try saying it in one sentence.</p></details>", "text": "python # hello.py name = input ( \"name? \" ) if name : print ( f \"hello, { name } !\" ) else : print ( \"hello, stranger!\" ) java (what you already know) public class hello { public static void main ( string [] args ) { scanner sc = new scanner ( system . in ); system . out . print ( \"name? \" ); string name = sc . nextline (); if ( ! name . isempty ()) { system . out . println ( \"hello, \" + name + \"!\" ); } else { system . out . println ( \"hello, stranger!\" ); } } } typing: java checks types when it compiles. python checks them while it runs, so a type mistake shows up as a crash at that line. blocks: the colon plus 4 spaces replace { } . mixing tabs and spaces is an error. running: no javac step. python3 hello.py runs the file. empty string is falsy , so if name: works like !name.isempty() . 🔁 explain it back: why does python crash at runtime for a type mistake that java would catch earlier? point at the word that changes: when the types get checked. try saying it in one sentence."}, {"id": "py-w1-ex", "title": "Exercise: tip calculator", "src": "Exercise", "idea": "Rewrite a tiny Java program in Python: read a bill and a tip percent, print the total to 2 decimals.", "deep": "<p><strong>Try it:</strong> Ask for the bill and the tip percent, then print <code>Total: $23.45</code>.</p><details><summary>Hint 1 (nudge)</summary><p><code>input()</code> always returns a string, just like <code>sc.nextLine()</code>.</p></details><details><summary>Hint 2 (stronger)</summary><p>Wrap the input in <code>float(...)</code>. For 2 decimals, use <code>:.2f</code> inside an f-string.</p></details><details><summary>Answer</summary><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">bill</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"nb\">float</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nb\">input</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Bill? \"</span><span class=\"p\">))</span>\n<span class=\"n\">tip</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"nb\">float</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nb\">input</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Tip %? \"</span><span class=\"p\">))</span>\n<span class=\"n\">total</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">bill</span> <span class=\"o\">*</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span> <span class=\"o\">+</span> <span class=\"n\">tip</span> <span class=\"o\">/</span> <span class=\"mi\">100</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Total: $</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">total</span><span class=\"si\">:</span><span class=\"s2\">.2f</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span></code></pre></div></details>", "text": "try it: ask for the bill and the tip percent, then print total: $23.45 . hint 1 (nudge) input() always returns a string, just like sc.nextline() . hint 2 (stronger) wrap the input in float(...) . for 2 decimals, use :.2f inside an f-string. answer bill = float ( input ( \"bill? \" )) tip = float ( input ( \"tip %? \" )) total = bill * ( 1 + tip / 100 ) print ( f \"total: $ { total : .2f } \" )"}]}, {"id": "py-w2", "title": "Week 2 · Variables, numbers and strings", "blurb": "Python's built-in types and how they map to Java's. ~4 hrs.", "topics": [{"id": "py-w2-plan", "title": "This week's plan", "src": "Course plan", "idea": "Numbers, strings, f-strings, and the gotchas that trip up Java programmers.", "deep": "<p>About <strong>3.5 hours</strong> this week, in 5 sessions. Do one per sitting.</p><ol><li><strong>35 min:</strong> Numbers: int has no size limit, / vs //</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> Strings: slicing, methods, immutability</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> f-strings and formatting</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> learnpython.org: Basic Operators + String Formatting</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> Exercise + explain-it-back question</li></ol><p>Reading: <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/introduction.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tutorial 3.1</a>.</p>", "text": "about 3.5 hours this week, in 5 sessions. do one per sitting. 35 min: numbers: int has no size limit, / vs // 40 min: strings: slicing, methods, immutability 35 min: f-strings and formatting 40 min: learnpython.org: basic operators + string formatting 40 min: exercise + explain-it-back question reading: tutorial 3.1 ."}, {"id": "py-w2-div", "title": "Division: / vs //", "src": "Tutorial 3.1.1", "idea": "In Python, / always gives a float, even 6 / 3. Use // when you want Java's integer division.", "deep": "<p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Python</strong></p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"mi\">7</span> <span class=\"o\">/</span> <span class=\"mi\">2</span>     <span class=\"c1\"># 3.5</span>\n<span class=\"mi\">7</span> <span class=\"o\">//</span> <span class=\"mi\">2</span>    <span class=\"c1\"># 3</span>\n<span class=\"o\">-</span><span class=\"mi\">7</span> <span class=\"o\">//</span> <span class=\"mi\">2</span>   <span class=\"c1\"># -4  (rounds down, not toward zero)</span>\n<span class=\"mi\">2</span> <span class=\"o\">**</span> <span class=\"mi\">100</span>  <span class=\"c1\"># works: ints never overflow</span></code></pre></div><p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Java</strong> (what you already know)</p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"mi\">7</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\">     </span><span class=\"c1\">// 3   (int / int)</span>\n<span class=\"mi\">7</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">2.0</span><span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"c1\">// 3.5</span>\n<span class=\"o\">-</span><span class=\"mi\">7</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"c1\">// -3  (rounds toward zero)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">Math</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">pow</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">100</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">// a double, precision lost</span></code></pre></div><p>The <code>-7 // 2</code> line is the classic trap: Python rounds toward negative infinity. <span class=\"by\">Added by Fern</span></p><details><summary>🔁 Explain it back: what does -7 // 2 give in Python, and why is it different from Java?</summary><p>Think about the direction each language rounds.</p></details>", "text": "python 7 / 2 # 3.5 7 // 2 # 3 - 7 // 2 # -4 (rounds down, not toward zero) 2 ** 100 # works: ints never overflow java (what you already know) 7 / 2 // 3 (int / int) 7 / 2.0 // 3.5 - 7 / 2 // -3 (rounds toward zero) math . pow ( 2 , 100 ) // a double, precision lost the -7 // 2 line is the classic trap: python rounds toward negative infinity. added by fern 🔁 explain it back: what does -7 // 2 give in python, and why is it different from java? think about the direction each language rounds."}, {"id": "py-w2-slice", "title": "Slicing strings", "src": "Tutorial 3.1.2", "idea": "s[start:end] gives a substring, and negative indexes count from the end. It replaces most uses of Java's substring().", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">s</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"fern-study\"</span>\n<span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>      <span class=\"c1\"># 'f'</span>\n<span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"o\">-</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>     <span class=\"c1\"># 'y'</span>\n<span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>    <span class=\"c1\"># 'fern'  (end is excluded, like substring)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"p\">:]</span>     <span class=\"c1\"># 'study'</span>\n<span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"p\">[::</span><span class=\"o\">-</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>   <span class=\"c1\"># 'yduts-nref'  (reversed)</span></code></pre></div>", "text": "s = \"fern-study\" s [ 0 ] # 'f' s [ - 1 ] # 'y' s [ 0 : 4 ] # 'fern' (end is excluded, like substring) s [ 5 :] # 'study' s [:: - 1 ] # 'yduts-nref' (reversed)"}, {"id": "py-w2-vars", "title": "Variables and naming", "src": "Tutorial 3.1", "idea": "A variable is just a name stuck on a value. No declaration and no type, and the same name can later hold a different type.", "deep": "<p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Python</strong></p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">count</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"mi\">3</span>\n<span class=\"n\">count</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"three\"</span>        <span class=\"c1\"># allowed: the name now points at a str</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">type</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">count</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>            <span class=\"c1\"># &lt;class 'str'&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"n\">user_name</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"noah\"</span>     <span class=\"c1\"># snake_case, not camelCase</span>\n<span class=\"n\">MAX_TRIES</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"mi\">5</span>          <span class=\"c1\"># 'constant' by convention only</span>\n<span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">b</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">2</span>            <span class=\"c1\"># two at once</span></code></pre></div><p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Java</strong> (what you already know)</p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kt\">int</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">count</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"n\">count</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s\">\"three\"</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\">       </span><span class=\"c1\">// compile error</span>\n<span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">userName</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s\">\"noah\"</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">final</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">int</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MAX_TRIES</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"p\">;</span></code></pre></div><p>Python's main types: <code>int</code>, <code>float</code>, <code>str</code>, <code>bool</code> (<code>True</code>/<code>False</code>, capitalized) and <code>None</code> (Python's <code>null</code>). There's no <code>char</code>. A single character is just a string of length 1.</p>", "text": "python count = 3 count = \"three\" # allowed: the name now points at a str type ( count ) # <class 'str'> user_name = \"noah\" # snake_case, not camelcase max_tries = 5 # 'constant' by convention only a , b = 1 , 2 # two at once java (what you already know) int count = 3 ; count = \"three\" ; // compile error string username = \"noah\" ; final int max_tries = 5 ; python's main types: int , float , str , bool ( true / false , capitalized) and none (python's null ). there's no char . a single character is just a string of length 1."}, {"id": "py-w2-strmeth", "title": "String methods and immutability", "src": "Library: str", "idea": "Strings can't be changed in place, just like Java. Methods return a new string, so save the result.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">s</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"  Fern Study  \"</span>\n<span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">strip</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>              <span class=\"c1\"># 'Fern Study'</span>\n<span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">lower</span><span class=\"p\">(),</span> <span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">upper</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>\n<span class=\"s2\">\"a,b,c\"</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">split</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\",\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>     <span class=\"c1\"># ['a', 'b', 'c']</span>\n<span class=\"s2\">\"-\"</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">join</span><span class=\"p\">([</span><span class=\"s2\">\"a\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"b\"</span><span class=\"p\">])</span>   <span class=\"c1\"># 'a-b'</span>\n<span class=\"s2\">\"fern\"</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">replace</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"f\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"F\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"s2\">\"study\"</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">s</span>           <span class=\"c1\"># False: case matters</span>\n<span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">strip</span><span class=\"p\">()</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">lower</span><span class=\"p\">()</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">startswith</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"fern\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>   <span class=\"c1\"># True</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">upper</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>              <span class=\"c1\"># trap: s itself didn't change</span></code></pre></div><p>Compare strings with <code>==</code> in Python. It compares contents, unlike <code>==</code> on Java objects, so there's no <code>.equals()</code> to remember. <span class=\"by\">Added by Fern</span></p>", "text": "s = \" fern study \" s . strip () # 'fern study' s . lower (), s . upper () \"a,b,c\" . split ( \",\" ) # ['a', 'b', 'c'] \"-\" . join ([ \"a\" , \"b\" ]) # 'a-b' \"fern\" . replace ( \"f\" , \"f\" ) \"study\" in s # false: case matters s . strip () . lower () . startswith ( \"fern\" ) # true s . upper () # trap: s itself didn't change compare strings with == in python. it compares contents, unlike == on java objects, so there's no .equals() to remember. added by fern"}, {"id": "py-w2-fstr", "title": "f-strings and formatting", "src": "Tutorial 7.1.1", "idea": "Put f before a string and anything in { } gets filled in. A format spec after a colon controls decimals, width and alignment.", "deep": "<p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Python</strong></p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">gpa</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"Noah\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mf\">3.8567</span>\n<span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\"> has a </span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">gpa</span><span class=\"si\">:</span><span class=\"s2\">.2f</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\"> GPA\"</span>     <span class=\"c1\"># 'Noah has a 3.86 GPA'</span>\n<span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"mi\">42</span><span class=\"si\">:</span><span class=\"s2\">&gt;6</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>                        <span class=\"c1\"># '    42'  (right, width 6)</span>\n<span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"mf\">0.25</span><span class=\"si\">:</span><span class=\"s2\">.0%</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>                     <span class=\"c1\"># '25%'</span>\n<span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"mi\">1234567</span><span class=\"si\">:</span><span class=\"s2\">,</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>                    <span class=\"c1\"># '1,234,567'</span>\n<span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"si\">=}</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>                        <span class=\"c1\"># \"name='Noah'\"  (debug)</span></code></pre></div><p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Java</strong> (what you already know)</p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">format</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"%s has a %.2f GPA\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">gpa</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">format</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"%6d\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">42</span><span class=\"p\">);</span></code></pre></div>", "text": "python name , gpa = \"noah\" , 3.8567 f \" { name } has a { gpa : .2f } gpa\" # 'noah has a 3.86 gpa' f \" { 42 : >6 } \" # ' 42' (right, width 6) f \" { 0.25 : .0% } \" # '25%' f \" { 1234567 : , } \" # '1,234,567' f \" { name =} \" # \"name='noah'\" (debug) java (what you already know) string . format ( \"%s has a %.2f gpa\" , name , gpa ); string . format ( \"%6d\" , 42 );"}, {"id": "py-w2-ex", "title": "Exercise: username maker", "src": "Exercise", "idea": "Ask for a first and last name and print a username: first initial plus last name, lowercase, with spaces removed, plus the name length.", "deep": "<p><strong>Try it:</strong> For <code>Noah</code> and <code>Van Hoag</code>, print <code>nvanhoag (8 chars)</code>.</p><details><summary>Hint 1 (nudge)</summary><p>Index 0 gets the first letter. Then think about which string methods clean up case and spaces.</p></details><details><summary>Hint 2 (stronger)</summary><p>Use <code>first[0]</code>, <code>.lower()</code>, <code>.replace(\" \", \"\")</code> and <code>len()</code>, and put it all in an f-string.</p></details><details><summary>Answer</summary><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">first</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"nb\">input</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"First name: \"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">strip</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>\n<span class=\"n\">last</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"nb\">input</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Last name: \"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">strip</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>\n<span class=\"n\">user</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">first</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">]</span> <span class=\"o\">+</span> <span class=\"n\">last</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">lower</span><span class=\"p\">()</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">replace</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\" \"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">user</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\"> (</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"nb\">len</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">user</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\"> chars)\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span></code></pre></div></details><details><summary>🔁 Explain it back: why doesn't s.upper() change s?</summary><p>Think about the word immutable, and what Java's String does in the same situation.</p></details>", "text": "try it: for noah and van hoag , print nvanhoag (8 chars) . hint 1 (nudge) index 0 gets the first letter. then think about which string methods clean up case and spaces. hint 2 (stronger) use first[0] , .lower() , .replace(\" \", \"\") and len() , and put it all in an f-string. answer first = input ( \"first name: \" ) . strip () last = input ( \"last name: \" ) . strip () user = ( first [ 0 ] + last ) . lower () . replace ( \" \" , \"\" ) print ( f \" { user } ( { len ( user ) } chars)\" ) 🔁 explain it back: why doesn't s.upper() change s? think about the word immutable, and what java's string does in the same situation."}]}, {"id": "py-w3", "title": "Week 3 · Control flow", "blurb": "if/elif/else, for with range(), while, and match. ~4 hrs.", "topics": [{"id": "py-w3-plan", "title": "This week's plan", "src": "Course plan", "idea": "if/elif/else, for with range(), while, and match.", "deep": "<p>About <strong>3.7 hours</strong> this week, in 6 sessions. Do one per sitting.</p><ol><li><strong>35 min:</strong> if/elif/else and truthiness</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> for loops and range()</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> while, break, continue</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> match statements</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> learnpython.org: Conditions + Loops</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> Exercise + explain-it-back question</li></ol><p>Reading: <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/controlflow.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tutorial 4.1–4.7</a>, <a href=\"https://www.learnpython.org/en/Conditions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">learnpython.org: Conditions</a>, <a href=\"https://www.learnpython.org/en/Loops\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Loops</a>.</p>", "text": "about 3.7 hours this week, in 6 sessions. do one per sitting. 35 min: if/elif/else and truthiness 40 min: for loops and range() 35 min: while, break, continue 35 min: match statements 40 min: learnpython.org: conditions + loops 35 min: exercise + explain-it-back question reading: tutorial 4.1–4.7 , learnpython.org: conditions , loops ."}, {"id": "py-w3-if", "title": "if, elif, else", "src": "Tutorial 4.1", "idea": "Same logic as Java, with elif instead of else if, a colon, and no parentheses or braces. and/or/not replace &&, || and !.", "deep": "<p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Python</strong></p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">score</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"mi\">83</span>\n<span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"n\">score</span> <span class=\"o\">&gt;=</span> <span class=\"mi\">90</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">grade</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"A\"</span>\n<span class=\"k\">elif</span> <span class=\"n\">score</span> <span class=\"o\">&gt;=</span> <span class=\"mi\">80</span> <span class=\"ow\">and</span> <span class=\"n\">score</span> <span class=\"o\">&lt;</span> <span class=\"mi\">90</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">grade</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"B\"</span>\n<span class=\"k\">else</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">grade</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"C\"</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\"># one-line version (ternary)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">label</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"pass\"</span> <span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"n\">score</span> <span class=\"o\">&gt;=</span> <span class=\"mi\">60</span> <span class=\"k\">else</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"fail\"</span></code></pre></div><p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Java</strong> (what you already know)</p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kt\">int</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">score</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">83</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">grade</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"k\">if</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">score</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&gt;=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">90</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">grade</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s\">\"A\"</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">else</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">if</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">score</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&gt;=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">80</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&amp;&amp;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">score</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">90</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">grade</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s\">\"B\"</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">else</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">grade</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s\">\"C\"</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">label</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">score</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&gt;=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">60</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">?</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s\">\"pass\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s\">\"fail\"</span><span class=\"p\">;</span></code></pre></div><ul><li>Python allows chained comparisons: <code>80 &lt;= score &lt; 90</code> works and reads like math.</li><li>The ternary reads in English order: <em>value</em> if <em>condition</em> else <em>other</em>.</li></ul>", "text": "python score = 83 if score >= 90 : grade = \"a\" elif score >= 80 and score < 90 : grade = \"b\" else : grade = \"c\" # one-line version (ternary) label = \"pass\" if score >= 60 else \"fail\" java (what you already know) int score = 83 ; string grade ; if ( score >= 90 ) { grade = \"a\" ; } else if ( score >= 80 && score < 90 ) { grade = \"b\" ; } else { grade = \"c\" ; } string label = score >= 60 ? \"pass\" : \"fail\" ; python allows chained comparisons: 80 <= score < 90 works and reads like math. the ternary reads in english order: value if condition else other ."}, {"id": "py-w3-truthy", "title": "Truthy and falsy values", "src": "Tutorial 4.1", "idea": "Any value can go in an if. Empty things and zero count as False: 0, 0.0, \"\", [], {}, set() and None.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">items</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">[]</span>\n<span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"ow\">not</span> <span class=\"n\">items</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>              <span class=\"c1\"># instead of items.size() == 0</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Nothing here\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">name</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"kc\">None</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">name</span> <span class=\"ow\">or</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"Guest\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>     <span class=\"c1\"># 'Guest': or returns the first truthy value</span></code></pre></div><p>Java only allows a real <code>boolean</code> in an <code>if</code>. Python lets you write <code>if items:</code>, which is the normal style. <span class=\"by\">Added by Fern</span></p>", "text": "items = [] if not items : # instead of items.size() == 0 print ( \"nothing here\" ) name = none print ( name or \"guest\" ) # 'guest': or returns the first truthy value java only allows a real boolean in an if . python lets you write if items: , which is the normal style. added by fern"}, {"id": "py-w3-for", "title": "for loops and range()", "src": "Tutorial 4.2–4.3", "idea": "Python's for always loops over something, like Java's for-each. For counting, loop over range(start, stop, step), where stop is excluded.", "deep": "<p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Python</strong></p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">i</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"nb\">range</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>          <span class=\"c1\"># 0 1 2 3 4</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">i</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"nb\">range</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">10</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"o\">-</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>  <span class=\"c1\"># 10 8 6 4 2</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">word</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"s2\">\"hi\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"there\"</span><span class=\"p\">]:</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">word</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">word</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"nb\">enumerate</span><span class=\"p\">([</span><span class=\"s2\">\"hi\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"there\"</span><span class=\"p\">]):</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">word</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>          <span class=\"c1\"># 0 hi / 1 there</span></code></pre></div><p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Java</strong> (what you already know)</p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"k\">for</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"kt\">int</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">++</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">System</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">out</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">println</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"k\">for</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"kt\">int</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">10</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">-=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">System</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">out</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">println</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"k\">for</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">word</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">of</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"hi\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s\">\"there\"</span><span class=\"p\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">System</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">out</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">println</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">word</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span></code></pre></div><p>Use <code>enumerate()</code> when you need the index and the item together. Avoid <code>for i in range(len(items))</code>.</p>", "text": "python for i in range ( 5 ): # 0 1 2 3 4 print ( i ) for i in range ( 10 , 0 , - 2 ): # 10 8 6 4 2 print ( i ) for word in [ \"hi\" , \"there\" ]: print ( word ) for i , word in enumerate ([ \"hi\" , \"there\" ]): print ( i , word ) # 0 hi / 1 there java (what you already know) for ( int i = 0 ; i < 5 ; i ++ ) { system . out . println ( i ); } for ( int i = 10 ; i > 0 ; i -= 2 ) { system . out . println ( i ); } for ( string word : list . of ( \"hi\" , \"there\" )) { system . out . println ( word ); } use enumerate() when you need the index and the item together. avoid for i in range(len(items)) ."}, {"id": "py-w3-while", "title": "while, break, continue", "src": "Tutorial 4.4–4.5", "idea": "while works like Java. Python has no do-while and no i++, so write i += 1. Loops can also have an else that runs if no break happened.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">tries</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span>\n<span class=\"k\">while</span> <span class=\"kc\">True</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>                 <span class=\"c1\"># Python's stand-in for do-while</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">guess</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"nb\">input</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Password? \"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">tries</span> <span class=\"o\">+=</span> <span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"n\">guess</span> <span class=\"o\">==</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"fern\"</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">break</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"n\">tries</span> <span class=\"o\">==</span> <span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n        <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Locked out\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">break</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">n</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">7</span><span class=\"p\">]:</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"n\">n</span> <span class=\"o\">%</span> <span class=\"mi\">2</span> <span class=\"o\">==</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n        <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"found an even number\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">break</span>\n<span class=\"k\">else</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>                       <span class=\"c1\"># runs only if the loop never hit break</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"no even numbers\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span></code></pre></div><p><code>pass</code> is a placeholder statement that does nothing. Use it for an empty block, where Java would use <code>{}</code>.</p>", "text": "tries = 0 while true : # python's stand-in for do-while guess = input ( \"password? \" ) tries += 1 if guess == \"fern\" : break if tries == 3 : print ( \"locked out\" ) break for n in [ 3 , 5 , 7 ]: if n % 2 == 0 : print ( \"found an even number\" ) break else : # runs only if the loop never hit break print ( \"no even numbers\" ) pass is a placeholder statement that does nothing. use it for an empty block, where java would use {} ."}, {"id": "py-w3-match", "title": "match: Python's switch", "src": "Tutorial 4.7", "idea": "match/case (Python 3.10+) works like Java's newer switch. No fall-through and no break. Use case _ for default.", "deep": "<p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Python</strong></p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">command</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"go north\"</span>\n<span class=\"k\">match</span> <span class=\"n\">command</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">split</span><span class=\"p\">():</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">case</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"s2\">\"quit\"</span><span class=\"p\">]:</span>\n        <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Bye\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">case</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"s2\">\"go\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">direction</span><span class=\"p\">]:</span>\n        <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Going\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">direction</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">case</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">_</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n        <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Unknown command\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span></code></pre></div><p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Java</strong> (what you already know)</p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"k\">switch</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">command</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">case</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s\">\"quit\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">-&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">System</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">out</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">println</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"Bye\"</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">case</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s\">\"help\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">-&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">System</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">out</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">println</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"Try: go north\"</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">default</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">-&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">System</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">out</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">println</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"Unknown command\"</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span></code></pre></div><p>match can also pull values out of lists and objects (\"pattern matching\"). Here <code>direction</code> captures the second word.</p>", "text": "python command = \"go north\" match command . split (): case [ \"quit\" ]: print ( \"bye\" ) case [ \"go\" , direction ]: print ( \"going\" , direction ) case _ : print ( \"unknown command\" ) java (what you already know) switch ( command ) { case \"quit\" -> system . out . println ( \"bye\" ); case \"help\" -> system . out . println ( \"try: go north\" ); default -> system . out . println ( \"unknown command\" ); } match can also pull values out of lists and objects (\"pattern matching\"). here direction captures the second word."}, {"id": "py-w3-ex", "title": "Exercise: number guessing game", "src": "Exercise", "idea": "Pick a random number from 1 to 100. Let the player guess and answer higher or lower until they get it, then print how many tries it took.", "deep": "<p><strong>Try it:</strong> Build the guessing game. <code>import random</code> and <code>random.randint(1, 100)</code> pick the number.</p><details><summary>Hint 1 (nudge)</summary><p>You don't know how many guesses there will be. Which loop fits that?</p></details><details><summary>Hint 2 (stronger)</summary><p>Use <code>while True:</code>, compare with if/elif/else, and <code>break</code> when the guess is right. Keep a counter.</p></details><details><summary>Answer</summary><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">random</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">secret</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">random</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">randint</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">100</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">tries</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span>\n<span class=\"k\">while</span> <span class=\"kc\">True</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">guess</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"nb\">int</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nb\">input</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Guess 1-100: \"</span><span class=\"p\">))</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">tries</span> <span class=\"o\">+=</span> <span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"n\">guess</span> <span class=\"o\">&lt;</span> <span class=\"n\">secret</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n        <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Higher\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">elif</span> <span class=\"n\">guess</span> <span class=\"o\">&gt;</span> <span class=\"n\">secret</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n        <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Lower\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">else</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n        <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Got it in </span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">tries</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\"> tries!\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">break</span></code></pre></div></details><details><summary>🔁 Explain it back: why does for i in range(1, 10) stop at 9?</summary><p>Think about what the stop value means in range, and which Java loop condition it matches.</p></details>", "text": "try it: build the guessing game. import random and random.randint(1, 100) pick the number. hint 1 (nudge) you don't know how many guesses there will be. which loop fits that? hint 2 (stronger) use while true: , compare with if/elif/else, and break when the guess is right. keep a counter. answer import random secret = random . randint ( 1 , 100 ) tries = 0 while true : guess = int ( input ( \"guess 1-100: \" )) tries += 1 if guess < secret : print ( \"higher\" ) elif guess > secret : print ( \"lower\" ) else : print ( f \"got it in { tries } tries!\" ) break 🔁 explain it back: why does for i in range(1, 10) stop at 9? think about what the stop value means in range, and which java loop condition it matches."}]}, {"id": "py-w4", "title": "Week 4 · Functions", "blurb": "def, default and keyword arguments, *args and **kwargs, scope, and lambda. ~4 hrs.", "topics": [{"id": "py-w4-plan", "title": "This week's plan", "src": "Course plan", "idea": "def, default and keyword arguments, *args and **kwargs, scope, and lambda.", "deep": "<p>About <strong>3.7 hours</strong> this week, in 6 sessions. Do one per sitting.</p><ol><li><strong>40 min:</strong> def, return, docstrings</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> Default and keyword arguments</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> *args and **kwargs</li><li><strong>30 min:</strong> Scope: local and global</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> learnpython.org: Functions</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> Exercise + explain-it-back question</li></ol><p>Reading: <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/controlflow.html#defining-functions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tutorial 4.8–4.9</a>, <a href=\"https://www.learnpython.org/en/Functions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">learnpython.org: Functions</a>.</p>", "text": "about 3.7 hours this week, in 6 sessions. do one per sitting. 40 min: def, return, docstrings 40 min: default and keyword arguments 35 min: *args and **kwargs 30 min: scope: local and global 35 min: learnpython.org: functions 40 min: exercise + explain-it-back question reading: tutorial 4.8–4.9 , learnpython.org: functions ."}, {"id": "py-w4-def", "title": "Defining functions", "src": "Tutorial 4.8", "idea": "def name(params): starts a function. No return type and no static. A function with no return gives back None, Python's null.", "deep": "<p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Python</strong></p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">area</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">width</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">height</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"sd\">\"\"\"Return the area of a rectangle.\"\"\"</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"n\">width</span> <span class=\"o\">*</span> <span class=\"n\">height</span>\n\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">area</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"p\">))</span>      <span class=\"c1\"># 12</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">area</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"vm\">__doc__</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>    <span class=\"c1\"># the docstring</span></code></pre></div><p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Java</strong> (what you already know)</p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kd\">static</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">int</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">area</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"kt\">int</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">width</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">int</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">height</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"c1\">// Return the area of a rectangle.</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">return</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">width</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">height</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"n\">System</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">out</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">println</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">area</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"p\">));</span></code></pre></div><ul><li>The string in triple quotes right under <code>def</code> is a <strong>docstring</strong>, Python's Javadoc. <code>help(area)</code> shows it.</li><li>Functions can be defined at the top level of a file, outside any class.</li><li>A function can return several values as a tuple: <code>return low, high</code>.</li></ul>", "text": "python def area ( width , height ): \"\"\"return the area of a rectangle.\"\"\" return width * height print ( area ( 3 , 4 )) # 12 print ( area . __doc__ ) # the docstring java (what you already know) static int area ( int width , int height ) { // return the area of a rectangle. return width * height ; } system . out . println ( area ( 3 , 4 )); the string in triple quotes right under def is a docstring , python's javadoc. help(area) shows it. functions can be defined at the top level of a file, outside any class. a function can return several values as a tuple: return low, high ."}, {"id": "py-w4-args", "title": "Default and keyword arguments", "src": "Tutorial 4.9.1–4.9.2", "idea": "Give a parameter a default value, and callers can skip it or name it. This replaces most of Java's method overloading.", "deep": "<p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Python</strong></p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">greet</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">greeting</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Hello\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">punct</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"!\"</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">greeting</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">, </span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"si\">}{</span><span class=\"n\">punct</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">greet</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Noah\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>                    <span class=\"c1\"># Hello, Noah!</span>\n<span class=\"n\">greet</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Noah\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"Hey\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>             <span class=\"c1\"># Hey, Noah!</span>\n<span class=\"n\">greet</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Noah\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">punct</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"?\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>         <span class=\"c1\"># Hello, Noah?  (keyword arg)</span></code></pre></div><p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Java</strong> (what you already know)</p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">greet</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">return</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">greet</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s\">\"Hello\"</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">greet</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">greeting</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">return</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">greeting</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s\">\", \"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s\">\"!\"</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">// a new overload for every combination</span></code></pre></div><p><strong>Trap:</strong> never use a list or dict as a default, like <code>def add(x, items=[])</code>. The same list is shared by every call. Use <code>items=None</code> and create the list inside. <span class=\"by\">Added by Fern</span></p>", "text": "python def greet ( name , greeting = \"hello\" , punct = \"!\" ): return f \" { greeting } , { name }{ punct } \" greet ( \"noah\" ) # hello, noah! greet ( \"noah\" , \"hey\" ) # hey, noah! greet ( \"noah\" , punct = \"?\" ) # hello, noah? (keyword arg) java (what you already know) string greet ( string name ) { return greet ( name , \"hello\" ); } string greet ( string name , string greeting ) { return greeting + \", \" + name + \"!\" ; } // a new overload for every combination trap: never use a list or dict as a default, like def add(x, items=[]) . the same list is shared by every call. use items=none and create the list inside. added by fern"}, {"id": "py-w4-star", "title": "*args and **kwargs", "src": "Tutorial 4.9.4", "idea": "*args collects extra positional arguments into a tuple, like Java's varargs. **kwargs collects extra named arguments into a dict.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">total</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"o\">*</span><span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"nb\">sum</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">total</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>          <span class=\"c1\"># 6   (nums is the tuple (1, 2, 3))</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">profile</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"o\">**</span><span class=\"n\">info</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">info</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">profile</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Noah\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">major</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"CS\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">school</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"UMGC\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"c1\"># Noah {'major': 'CS', 'school': 'UMGC'}</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">values</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>\n<span class=\"n\">total</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"o\">*</span><span class=\"n\">values</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>          <span class=\"c1\"># * also unpacks a list into arguments</span></code></pre></div><p>Java equivalent of <code>*nums</code>: <code>int total(int... nums)</code>. Java has nothing like <code>**kwargs</code>.</p>", "text": "def total ( * nums ): return sum ( nums ) total ( 1 , 2 , 3 ) # 6 (nums is the tuple (1, 2, 3)) def profile ( name , ** info ): print ( name , info ) profile ( \"noah\" , major = \"cs\" , school = \"umgc\" ) # noah {'major': 'cs', 'school': 'umgc'} values = [ 4 , 5 ] total ( * values ) # * also unpacks a list into arguments java equivalent of *nums : int total(int... nums) . java has nothing like **kwargs ."}, {"id": "py-w4-scope", "title": "Scope and global", "src": "Tutorial 9.2", "idea": "Variables made inside a function are local. A function can read a global variable, but it needs the global keyword to change it.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">count</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">bump</span><span class=\"p\">():</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">global</span> <span class=\"n\">count</span>      <span class=\"c1\"># without this line: UnboundLocalError</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">count</span> <span class=\"o\">+=</span> <span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">bump</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">count</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>          <span class=\"c1\"># 1</span></code></pre></div><ul><li>An <code>if</code> or <code>for</code> block does <strong>not</strong> create a new scope in Python. A variable set inside a loop still exists after it, unlike Java.</li><li>Using <code>global</code> a lot is a code smell. Pass values in and return them instead.</li></ul>", "text": "count = 0 def bump (): global count # without this line: unboundlocalerror count += 1 bump () print ( count ) # 1 an if or for block does not create a new scope in python. a variable set inside a loop still exists after it, unlike java. using global a lot is a code smell. pass values in and return them instead."}, {"id": "py-w4-lambda", "title": "lambda: tiny anonymous functions", "src": "Tutorial 4.9.6", "idea": "lambda args: expression makes a one-line function. It's like a Java lambda, and it's mostly used as a key for sorting.", "deep": "<p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Python</strong></p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">words</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"s2\">\"pear\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"fig\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"banana\"</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>\n<span class=\"n\">words</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">sort</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">key</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"k\">lambda</span> <span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"nb\">len</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"p\">))</span>\n<span class=\"c1\"># ['fig', 'pear', 'banana']</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">square</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"k\">lambda</span> <span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"n\">x</span> <span class=\"o\">*</span> <span class=\"n\">x</span>   <span class=\"c1\"># works, but def is clearer</span></code></pre></div><p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Java</strong> (what you already know)</p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"o\">&lt;</span><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"o\">&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">words</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">new</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ArrayList</span><span class=\"o\">&lt;&gt;</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">of</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"pear\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s\">\"fig\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s\">\"banana\"</span><span class=\"p\">));</span>\n<span class=\"n\">words</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">sort</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Comparator</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">comparing</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">-&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">length</span><span class=\"p\">()));</span></code></pre></div>", "text": "python words = [ \"pear\" , \"fig\" , \"banana\" ] words . sort ( key = lambda w : len ( w )) # ['fig', 'pear', 'banana'] square = lambda x : x * x # works, but def is clearer java (what you already know) list < string > words = new arraylist <> ( list . of ( \"pear\" , \"fig\" , \"banana\" )); words . sort ( comparator . comparing ( w -> w . length ()));"}, {"id": "py-w4-ex", "title": "Exercise: refactor the guessing game", "src": "Exercise", "idea": "Split week 3's game into functions: one to get a valid guess, one to play a round, and one to ask about playing again.", "deep": "<p><strong>Try it:</strong> Refactor the game so the main loop is only a few lines. Give the max number a default of 100.</p><details><summary>Hint 1 (nudge)</summary><p>Think about what one function should do, then name it with a verb.</p></details><details><summary>Hint 2 (stronger)</summary><p>Try <code>get_guess(low, high)</code> that loops until the input is a number in range, <code>play(max_n=100)</code> that returns the try count, and a <code>while</code> loop that calls <code>play()</code>.</p></details><details><summary>Answer</summary><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">random</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">get_guess</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">low</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">high</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">while</span> <span class=\"kc\">True</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n        <span class=\"n\">text</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"nb\">input</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Guess </span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">low</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">-</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">high</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">: \"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"n\">text</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">isdigit</span><span class=\"p\">()</span> <span class=\"ow\">and</span> <span class=\"n\">low</span> <span class=\"o\">&lt;=</span> <span class=\"nb\">int</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">text</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"o\">&lt;=</span> <span class=\"n\">high</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n            <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"nb\">int</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">text</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n        <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Please enter a number in range.\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">play</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">max_n</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"mi\">100</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">secret</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">random</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">randint</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">max_n</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">tries</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">while</span> <span class=\"kc\">True</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n        <span class=\"n\">guess</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">get_guess</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">max_n</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n        <span class=\"n\">tries</span> <span class=\"o\">+=</span> <span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"n\">guess</span> <span class=\"o\">==</span> <span class=\"n\">secret</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n            <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"n\">tries</span>\n        <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Higher\"</span> <span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"n\">guess</span> <span class=\"o\">&lt;</span> <span class=\"n\">secret</span> <span class=\"k\">else</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"Lower\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">while</span> <span class=\"kc\">True</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Got it in </span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">play</span><span class=\"p\">()</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\"> tries!\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"nb\">input</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Again? (y/n) \"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">lower</span><span class=\"p\">()</span> <span class=\"o\">!=</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"y\"</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">break</span></code></pre></div></details><details><summary>🔁 Explain it back: what does a function return if it never hits a return statement?</summary><p>Think about Python's version of null, and what <code>print(print(\"hi\"))</code> would show.</p></details>", "text": "try it: refactor the game so the main loop is only a few lines. give the max number a default of 100. hint 1 (nudge) think about what one function should do, then name it with a verb. hint 2 (stronger) try get_guess(low, high) that loops until the input is a number in range, play(max_n=100) that returns the try count, and a while loop that calls play() . answer import random def get_guess ( low , high ): while true : text = input ( f \"guess { low } - { high } : \" ) if text . isdigit () and low <= int ( text ) <= high : return int ( text ) print ( \"please enter a number in range.\" ) def play ( max_n = 100 ): secret = random . randint ( 1 , max_n ) tries = 0 while true : guess = get_guess ( 1 , max_n ) tries += 1 if guess == secret : return tries print ( \"higher\" if guess < secret else \"lower\" ) while true : print ( f \"got it in { play () } tries!\" ) if input ( \"again? (y/n) \" ) . lower () != \"y\" : break 🔁 explain it back: what does a function return if it never hits a return statement? think about python's version of null, and what print(print(\"hi\")) would show."}]}, {"id": "py-w5", "title": "Week 5 · Lists and tuples", "blurb": "Lists vs ArrayList, tuples, slicing, sorting, and list comprehensions. ~4 hrs.", "topics": [{"id": "py-w5-plan", "title": "This week's plan", "src": "Course plan", "idea": "Lists vs ArrayList, tuples, slicing, sorting, and list comprehensions.", "deep": "<p>About <strong>3.8 hours</strong> this week, in 6 sessions. Do one per sitting.</p><ol><li><strong>40 min:</strong> List basics</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> Tuples and unpacking</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> Sorting with key=</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> List comprehensions</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> learnpython.org: Lists + List Comprehensions</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> Exercise + explain-it-back question</li></ol><p>Reading: <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tutorial 5.1–5.3</a>, <a href=\"https://www.learnpython.org/en/Lists\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">learnpython.org: Lists</a>, <a href=\"https://www.learnpython.org/en/List_Comprehensions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">List Comprehensions</a>.</p>", "text": "about 3.8 hours this week, in 6 sessions. do one per sitting. 40 min: list basics 35 min: tuples and unpacking 40 min: sorting with key= 40 min: list comprehensions 35 min: learnpython.org: lists + list comprehensions 40 min: exercise + explain-it-back question reading: tutorial 5.1–5.3 , learnpython.org: lists , list comprehensions ."}, {"id": "py-w5-list", "title": "Lists: Python's ArrayList", "src": "Tutorial 5.1", "idea": "A list is written with [ ], grows and shrinks as needed, and can hold any mix of types. Indexing and slicing work just like strings.", "deep": "<p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Python</strong></p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">nums</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>\n<span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">append</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>        <span class=\"c1\"># [3, 1, 4, 1]</span>\n<span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">insert</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">9</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>     <span class=\"c1\"># [9, 3, 1, 4, 1]</span>\n<span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">pop</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>            <span class=\"c1\"># removes and returns the last item</span>\n<span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">remove</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>        <span class=\"c1\"># removes the first 3</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">len</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>             <span class=\"c1\"># size</span>\n<span class=\"mi\">4</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">nums</span>             <span class=\"c1\"># True  (contains)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"o\">-</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>              <span class=\"c1\"># last item</span></code></pre></div><p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Java</strong> (what you already know)</p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"o\">&lt;</span><span class=\"n\">Integer</span><span class=\"o\">&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">new</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ArrayList</span><span class=\"o\">&lt;&gt;</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">of</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"p\">));</span>\n<span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">add</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">add</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">9</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">remove</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">size</span><span class=\"p\">()</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">remove</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Integer</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">valueOf</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">));</span>\n<span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">size</span><span class=\"p\">();</span>\n<span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">contains</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">get</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">size</span><span class=\"p\">()</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">);</span></code></pre></div><p><strong>Copying:</strong> <code>b = a</code> does not copy a list. Both names point to the same list, just like Java references. Use <code>b = a.copy()</code> or <code>a[:]</code>.</p>", "text": "python nums = [ 3 , 1 , 4 ] nums . append ( 1 ) # [3, 1, 4, 1] nums . insert ( 0 , 9 ) # [9, 3, 1, 4, 1] nums . pop () # removes and returns the last item nums . remove ( 3 ) # removes the first 3 len ( nums ) # size 4 in nums # true (contains) nums [ - 1 ] # last item java (what you already know) list < integer > nums = new arraylist <> ( list . of ( 3 , 1 , 4 )); nums . add ( 1 ); nums . add ( 0 , 9 ); nums . remove ( nums . size () - 1 ); nums . remove ( integer . valueof ( 3 )); nums . size (); nums . contains ( 4 ); nums . get ( nums . size () - 1 ); copying: b = a does not copy a list. both names point to the same list, just like java references. use b = a.copy() or a[:] ."}, {"id": "py-w5-tuple", "title": "Tuples and unpacking", "src": "Tutorial 5.3", "idea": "A tuple is like a list that can't change, written with ( ). Unpacking assigns its parts to several variables at once.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">point</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">y</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">point</span>           <span class=\"c1\"># unpacking</span>\n<span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">b</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">a</span>            <span class=\"c1\"># swap two variables, no temp needed</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">min_max</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"nb\">min</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"p\">),</span> <span class=\"nb\">max</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>   <span class=\"c1\"># returns a tuple</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">low</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">high</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">min_max</span><span class=\"p\">([</span><span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">9</span><span class=\"p\">])</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">first</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"o\">*</span><span class=\"n\">rest</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>       <span class=\"c1\"># first=1, rest=[2, 3, 4]</span></code></pre></div><p>Use a tuple for a fixed group of values, like a coordinate or a row. Use a list for a collection that grows. Java has no built-in tuple. You'd write a <code>record</code>.</p>", "text": "point = ( 3 , 4 ) x , y = point # unpacking a , b = b , a # swap two variables, no temp needed def min_max ( nums ): return min ( nums ), max ( nums ) # returns a tuple low , high = min_max ([ 5 , 2 , 9 ]) first , * rest = [ 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 ] # first=1, rest=[2, 3, 4] use a tuple for a fixed group of values, like a coordinate or a row. use a list for a collection that grows. java has no built-in tuple. you'd write a record ."}, {"id": "py-w5-sort", "title": "Sorting", "src": "Sorting HOWTO", "idea": "list.sort() sorts in place and returns None. sorted(x) returns a new list. Both take key= and reverse=.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">scores</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">[(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Ana\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">88</span><span class=\"p\">),</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Ben\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">95</span><span class=\"p\">),</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Cy\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">72</span><span class=\"p\">)]</span>\n<span class=\"n\">by_score</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"nb\">sorted</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">scores</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">key</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"k\">lambda</span> <span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">],</span> <span class=\"n\">reverse</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"kc\">True</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"c1\"># [('Ben', 95), ('Ana', 88), ('Cy', 72)]</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">names</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"s2\">\"bo\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"Al\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"cy\"</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>\n<span class=\"n\">names</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">sort</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">key</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"nb\">str</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">lower</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>     <span class=\"c1\"># case-insensitive</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">x</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">names</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">sort</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>              <span class=\"c1\"># trap: x is None</span></code></pre></div><p>More in the <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/howto/sorting.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sorting HOWTO</a>.</p>", "text": "scores = [( \"ana\" , 88 ), ( \"ben\" , 95 ), ( \"cy\" , 72 )] by_score = sorted ( scores , key = lambda s : s [ 1 ], reverse = true ) # [('ben', 95), ('ana', 88), ('cy', 72)] names = [ \"bo\" , \"al\" , \"cy\" ] names . sort ( key = str . lower ) # case-insensitive x = names . sort () # trap: x is none more in the sorting howto ."}, {"id": "py-w5-comp", "title": "List comprehensions", "src": "Tutorial 5.1.3", "idea": "[expr for item in items if condition] builds a new list in one line. It's Python's version of a Java stream with map and filter.", "deep": "<p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Python</strong></p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">nums</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">6</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>\n<span class=\"n\">squares</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"n\">n</span> <span class=\"o\">*</span> <span class=\"n\">n</span> <span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">n</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>\n<span class=\"n\">evens</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"n\">n</span> <span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">n</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">nums</span> <span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"n\">n</span> <span class=\"o\">%</span> <span class=\"mi\">2</span> <span class=\"o\">==</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>\n<span class=\"n\">words</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">upper</span><span class=\"p\">()</span> <span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">w</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"s2\">\"hi\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"yo\"</span><span class=\"p\">]]</span></code></pre></div><p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Java</strong> (what you already know)</p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"o\">&lt;</span><span class=\"n\">Integer</span><span class=\"o\">&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">squares</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">stream</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">map</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">-&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">toList</span><span class=\"p\">();</span>\n<span class=\"n\">List</span><span class=\"o\">&lt;</span><span class=\"n\">Integer</span><span class=\"o\">&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">evens</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">stream</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">filter</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">-&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">%</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">==</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">toList</span><span class=\"p\">();</span></code></pre></div><p>If a comprehension needs more than one <code>if</code> or a nested loop, a plain <code>for</code> loop is usually easier to read.</p>", "text": "python nums = [ 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 ] squares = [ n * n for n in nums ] evens = [ n for n in nums if n % 2 == 0 ] words = [ w . upper () for w in [ \"hi\" , \"yo\" ]] java (what you already know) list < integer > squares = nums . stream () . map ( n -> n * n ) . tolist (); list < integer > evens = nums . stream () . filter ( n -> n % 2 == 0 ) . tolist (); if a comprehension needs more than one if or a nested loop, a plain for loop is usually easier to read."}, {"id": "py-w5-ex", "title": "Exercise: grade stats", "src": "Exercise", "idea": "Given a list of scores, print the average, the highest and lowest, and a list of only the passing scores (60 or higher).", "deep": "<p><strong>Try it:</strong> Use <code>scores = [88, 52, 95, 71, 60, 43]</code>.</p><details><summary>Hint 1 (nudge)</summary><p>Python has built-in functions for most of this. No loops needed.</p></details><details><summary>Hint 2 (stronger)</summary><p>Try <code>sum()</code>, <code>len()</code>, <code>max()</code> and <code>min()</code>, plus a list comprehension with an <code>if</code>.</p></details><details><summary>Answer</summary><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">scores</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">88</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">52</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">95</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">71</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">60</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">43</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>\n<span class=\"n\">avg</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"nb\">sum</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">scores</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"o\">/</span> <span class=\"nb\">len</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">scores</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">passing</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"n\">s</span> <span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">s</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">scores</span> <span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"n\">s</span> <span class=\"o\">&gt;=</span> <span class=\"mi\">60</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Average: </span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">avg</span><span class=\"si\">:</span><span class=\"s2\">.1f</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"High </span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"nb\">max</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">scores</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">, low </span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"nb\">min</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">scores</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Passing:\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">passing</span><span class=\"p\">)</span></code></pre></div></details><details><summary>🔁 Explain it back: after b = a and then b.append(1), why does a change too?</summary><p>Think about what a variable holds in Java when the value is an object.</p></details>", "text": "try it: use scores = [88, 52, 95, 71, 60, 43] . hint 1 (nudge) python has built-in functions for most of this. no loops needed. hint 2 (stronger) try sum() , len() , max() and min() , plus a list comprehension with an if . answer scores = [ 88 , 52 , 95 , 71 , 60 , 43 ] avg = sum ( scores ) / len ( scores ) passing = [ s for s in scores if s >= 60 ] print ( f \"average: { avg : .1f } \" ) print ( f \"high { max ( scores ) } , low { min ( scores ) } \" ) print ( \"passing:\" , passing ) 🔁 explain it back: after b = a and then b.append(1), why does a change too? think about what a variable holds in java when the value is an object."}]}, {"id": "py-w6", "title": "Week 6 · Dicts and sets", "blurb": "Dictionaries vs HashMap, sets vs HashSet, and looping with items(). ~4 hrs.", "topics": [{"id": "py-w6-plan", "title": "This week's plan", "src": "Course plan", "idea": "Dictionaries vs HashMap, sets vs HashSet, and looping with items().", "deep": "<p>About <strong>3.7 hours</strong> this week, in 6 sessions. Do one per sitting.</p><ol><li><strong>40 min:</strong> Dicts vs HashMap</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> get(), items() and looping</li><li><strong>30 min:</strong> Sets vs HashSet</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> Dict and set comprehensions</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> learnpython.org: Dictionaries + Sets</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> Exercise + explain-it-back question</li></ol><p>Reading: <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#sets\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tutorial 5.4–5.6</a>, <a href=\"https://www.learnpython.org/en/Dictionaries\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">learnpython.org: Dictionaries</a>, <a href=\"https://www.learnpython.org/en/Sets\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sets</a>.</p>", "text": "about 3.7 hours this week, in 6 sessions. do one per sitting. 40 min: dicts vs hashmap 35 min: get(), items() and looping 30 min: sets vs hashset 40 min: dict and set comprehensions 35 min: learnpython.org: dictionaries + sets 40 min: exercise + explain-it-back question reading: tutorial 5.4–5.6 , learnpython.org: dictionaries , sets ."}, {"id": "py-w6-dict", "title": "Dicts: Python's HashMap", "src": "Tutorial 5.5", "idea": "A dict maps keys to values, written {key: value}. Read with d[key], write with d[key] = value. Since Python 3.7 it keeps insertion order.", "deep": "<p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Python</strong></p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">ages</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">{</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Ana\"</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"mi\">20</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"Ben\"</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"mi\">22</span><span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"n\">ages</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Cy\"</span><span class=\"p\">]</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"mi\">19</span>          <span class=\"c1\"># put</span>\n<span class=\"n\">ages</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Ana\"</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>              <span class=\"c1\"># get: 20</span>\n<span class=\"n\">ages</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Zed\"</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>              <span class=\"c1\"># KeyError!</span>\n<span class=\"n\">ages</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">get</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Zed\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>       <span class=\"c1\"># 0 (default instead of an error)</span>\n<span class=\"s2\">\"Ben\"</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">ages</span>            <span class=\"c1\"># True (containsKey)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">del</span> <span class=\"n\">ages</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Ben\"</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>          <span class=\"c1\"># remove</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">len</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ages</span><span class=\"p\">)</span></code></pre></div><p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Java</strong> (what you already know)</p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">Map</span><span class=\"o\">&lt;</span><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Integer</span><span class=\"o\">&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ages</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">new</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">HashMap</span><span class=\"o\">&lt;&gt;</span><span class=\"p\">();</span>\n<span class=\"n\">ages</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">put</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"Ana\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">20</span><span class=\"p\">);</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ages</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">put</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"Ben\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">22</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"n\">ages</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">put</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"Cy\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">19</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"n\">ages</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">get</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"Ana\"</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"n\">ages</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">get</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"Zed\"</span><span class=\"p\">);</span><span class=\"w\">             </span><span class=\"c1\">// null, no error</span>\n<span class=\"n\">ages</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">getOrDefault</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"Zed\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"n\">ages</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">containsKey</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"Ben\"</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"n\">ages</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">remove</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"Ben\"</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"n\">ages</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">size</span><span class=\"p\">();</span></code></pre></div><p>Big difference: a missing key raises <code>KeyError</code> in Python instead of returning null. Use <code>.get()</code> when the key might be missing.</p>", "text": "python ages = { \"ana\" : 20 , \"ben\" : 22 } ages [ \"cy\" ] = 19 # put ages [ \"ana\" ] # get: 20 ages [ \"zed\" ] # keyerror! ages . get ( \"zed\" , 0 ) # 0 (default instead of an error) \"ben\" in ages # true (containskey) del ages [ \"ben\" ] # remove len ( ages ) java (what you already know) map < string , integer > ages = new hashmap <> (); ages . put ( \"ana\" , 20 ); ages . put ( \"ben\" , 22 ); ages . put ( \"cy\" , 19 ); ages . get ( \"ana\" ); ages . get ( \"zed\" ); // null, no error ages . getordefault ( \"zed\" , 0 ); ages . containskey ( \"ben\" ); ages . remove ( \"ben\" ); ages . size (); big difference: a missing key raises keyerror in python instead of returning null. use .get() when the key might be missing."}, {"id": "py-w6-loop", "title": "Looping over a dict", "src": "Tutorial 5.6", "idea": "Looping over a dict gives its keys. Use .items() to get key and value together, like Java's entrySet().", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">prices</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">{</span><span class=\"s2\">\"apple\"</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"mf\">1.25</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"pear\"</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"mf\">0.9</span><span class=\"p\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">fruit</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">prices</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>                  <span class=\"c1\"># keys</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">fruit</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">fruit</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">price</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">prices</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">items</span><span class=\"p\">():</span>   <span class=\"c1\"># key and value</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">fruit</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">: $</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">price</span><span class=\"si\">:</span><span class=\"s2\">.2f</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"nb\">list</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">prices</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">values</span><span class=\"p\">())</span>                 <span class=\"c1\"># [1.25, 0.9]</span></code></pre></div>", "text": "prices = { \"apple\" : 1.25 , \"pear\" : 0.9 } for fruit in prices : # keys print ( fruit ) for fruit , price in prices . items (): # key and value print ( f \" { fruit } : $ { price : .2f } \" ) list ( prices . values ()) # [1.25, 0.9]"}, {"id": "py-w6-set", "title": "Sets: Python's HashSet", "src": "Tutorial 5.4", "idea": "A set holds unique items with no order, written {1, 2, 3}. It's great for removing duplicates and fast membership checks.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">tags</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">{</span><span class=\"s2\">\"java\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"python\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"java\"</span><span class=\"p\">}</span>   <span class=\"c1\"># {'java', 'python'}</span>\n<span class=\"n\">tags</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">add</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"sql\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"s2\">\"java\"</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">tags</span>                       <span class=\"c1\"># True, and fast</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">a</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">{</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"n\">b</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">{</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"n\">a</span> <span class=\"o\">|</span> <span class=\"n\">b</span>      <span class=\"c1\"># union        {1, 2, 3, 4}</span>\n<span class=\"n\">a</span> <span class=\"o\">&amp;</span> <span class=\"n\">b</span>      <span class=\"c1\"># intersection {2, 3}</span>\n<span class=\"n\">a</span> <span class=\"o\">-</span> <span class=\"n\">b</span>      <span class=\"c1\"># difference   {1}</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">empty</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"nb\">set</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>   <span class=\"c1\"># {} is an empty dict, not a set!</span></code></pre></div><p>Dict keys and set items must be <strong>hashable</strong>, which means they can't change. Strings, numbers and tuples work. Lists don't. Same idea as Java needing a stable <code>hashCode()</code>.</p>", "text": "tags = { \"java\" , \"python\" , \"java\" } # {'java', 'python'} tags . add ( \"sql\" ) \"java\" in tags # true, and fast a = { 1 , 2 , 3 } b = { 2 , 3 , 4 } a | b # union {1, 2, 3, 4} a & b # intersection {2, 3} a - b # difference {1} empty = set () # {} is an empty dict, not a set! dict keys and set items must be hashable , which means they can't change. strings, numbers and tuples work. lists don't. same idea as java needing a stable hashcode() ."}, {"id": "py-w6-comp", "title": "Dict and set comprehensions", "src": "Tutorial 5.5", "idea": "The same comprehension syntax builds dicts and sets: {k: v for ...} and {x for ...}.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">words</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"s2\">\"fern\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"moss\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"fig\"</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>\n<span class=\"n\">lengths</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">{</span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"nb\">len</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">w</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">words</span><span class=\"p\">}</span>   <span class=\"c1\"># {'fern': 4, 'moss': 4, 'fig': 3}</span>\n<span class=\"n\">first_letters</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">{</span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">]</span> <span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">w</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">words</span><span class=\"p\">}</span>  <span class=\"c1\"># {'f', 'm'}</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">from</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">collections</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">import</span> <span class=\"n\">Counter</span>        <span class=\"c1\"># preview of week 10</span>\n<span class=\"n\">Counter</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"banana\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>                      <span class=\"c1\"># Counter({'a': 3, 'n': 2, 'b': 1})</span></code></pre></div>", "text": "words = [ \"fern\" , \"moss\" , \"fig\" ] lengths = { w : len ( w ) for w in words } # {'fern': 4, 'moss': 4, 'fig': 3} first_letters = { w [ 0 ] for w in words } # {'f', 'm'} from collections import counter # preview of week 10 counter ( \"banana\" ) # counter({'a': 3, 'n': 2, 'b': 1})"}, {"id": "py-w6-ex", "title": "Exercise: word counter", "src": "Exercise", "idea": "Ask for a sentence and print how many times each word appears, most common first. Ignore upper and lower case.", "deep": "<p><strong>Try it:</strong> Count the words in <code>\"The cat and the hat and THE bat\"</code>.</p><details><summary>Hint 1 (nudge)</summary><p>Turn the sentence into a list of lowercase words first. Then a dict can map each word to its count.</p></details><details><summary>Hint 2 (stronger)</summary><p><code>sentence.lower().split()</code> gives the words. <code>counts[w] = counts.get(w, 0) + 1</code> counts them. Sort <code>counts.items()</code> by the count.</p></details><details><summary>Answer</summary><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">sentence</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"nb\">input</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Sentence: \"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">counts</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">{}</span>\n<span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">w</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">sentence</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">lower</span><span class=\"p\">()</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">split</span><span class=\"p\">():</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">counts</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"p\">]</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">counts</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">get</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"o\">+</span> <span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">word</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">n</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"nb\">sorted</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">counts</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">items</span><span class=\"p\">(),</span> <span class=\"n\">key</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"k\">lambda</span> <span class=\"n\">kv</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"n\">kv</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">],</span> <span class=\"n\">reverse</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"kc\">True</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">word</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"p\">)</span></code></pre></div></details><details><summary>🔁 Explain it back: why can't a list be a dict key, when a tuple can?</summary><p>Think about what would happen to the key's hash if you changed the list after storing it.</p></details>", "text": "try it: count the words in \"the cat and the hat and the bat\" . hint 1 (nudge) turn the sentence into a list of lowercase words first. then a dict can map each word to its count. hint 2 (stronger) sentence.lower().split() gives the words. counts[w] = counts.get(w, 0) + 1 counts them. sort counts.items() by the count. answer sentence = input ( \"sentence: \" ) counts = {} for w in sentence . lower () . split (): counts [ w ] = counts . get ( w , 0 ) + 1 for word , n in sorted ( counts . items (), key = lambda kv : kv [ 1 ], reverse = true ): print ( word , n ) 🔁 explain it back: why can't a list be a dict key, when a tuple can? think about what would happen to the key's hash if you changed the list after storing it."}]}, {"id": "py-w7", "title": "Week 7 · Modules, packages and pip", "blurb": "import, __name__ == \"__main__\", venv, and pip install. ~4 hrs.", "topics": [{"id": "py-w7-plan", "title": "This week's plan", "src": "Course plan", "idea": "import, __name__ == \"__main__\", venv, and pip install.", "deep": "<p>About <strong>3.8 hours</strong> this week, in 6 sessions. Do one per sitting.</p><ol><li><strong>35 min:</strong> import styles</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> Your own modules and __main__</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> Packages and folders</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> venv and pip</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> learnpython.org: Modules and Packages</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> Exercise + explain-it-back question</li></ol><p>Reading: <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/modules.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tutorial ch. 6</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/venv.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tutorial ch. 12 (venv)</a>, <a href=\"https://www.learnpython.org/en/Modules_and_Packages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">learnpython.org: Modules and Packages</a>.</p>", "text": "about 3.8 hours this week, in 6 sessions. do one per sitting. 35 min: import styles 35 min: your own modules and __main__ 40 min: packages and folders 40 min: venv and pip 35 min: learnpython.org: modules and packages 40 min: exercise + explain-it-back question reading: tutorial ch. 6 , tutorial ch. 12 (venv) , learnpython.org: modules and packages ."}, {"id": "py-w7-import", "title": "import", "src": "Tutorial 6", "idea": "Any .py file is a module. import loads it. Unlike Java, an import runs the module's top-level code once.", "deep": "<p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Python</strong></p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">math</span>\n<span class=\"n\">math</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">sqrt</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">16</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>                 <span class=\"c1\"># 4.0</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">from</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">math</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">import</span> <span class=\"n\">sqrt</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">pi</span>     <span class=\"c1\"># import specific names</span>\n<span class=\"n\">sqrt</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">16</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">datetime</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">as</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">dt</span>         <span class=\"c1\"># nickname</span>\n<span class=\"n\">dt</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">date</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">today</span><span class=\"p\">()</span></code></pre></div><p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Java</strong> (what you already know)</p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">java.lang.Math</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\">     </span><span class=\"c1\">// automatic anyway</span>\n<span class=\"n\">Math</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">sqrt</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">16</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">import static</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">java.lang.Math.sqrt</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"n\">sqrt</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">16</span><span class=\"p\">);</span></code></pre></div><p>Avoid <code>from module import *</code>. It hides where names come from, like a wildcard import in Java but worse.</p>", "text": "python import math math . sqrt ( 16 ) # 4.0 from math import sqrt , pi # import specific names sqrt ( 16 ) import datetime as dt # nickname dt . date . today () java (what you already know) import java.lang.math ; // automatic anyway math . sqrt ( 16 ); import static java.lang.math.sqrt ; sqrt ( 16 ); avoid from module import * . it hides where names come from, like a wildcard import in java but worse."}, {"id": "py-w7-main", "title": "if __name__ == \"__main__\"", "src": "Tutorial 6.1.1", "idea": "Each module has a __name__. It equals \"__main__\" only when the file is run directly, so this block is Python's main method.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"c1\"># game.py</span>\n<span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">play</span><span class=\"p\">():</span>\n    <span class=\"o\">...</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"vm\">__name__</span> <span class=\"o\">==</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"__main__\"</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">play</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>        <span class=\"c1\"># runs for: python3 game.py</span>\n                  <span class=\"c1\"># skipped for: import game</span></code></pre></div><p>This lets a file be both a script you run and a module other files import, without the game starting on import.</p>", "text": "# game.py def play (): ... if __name__ == \"__main__\" : play () # runs for: python3 game.py # skipped for: import game this lets a file be both a script you run and a module other files import, without the game starting on import."}, {"id": "py-w7-pkg", "title": "Packages: folders of modules", "src": "Tutorial 6.4", "idea": "A package is a folder of modules, usually with an __init__.py file. Import with dots, like Java packages.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code>aac/\n<span class=\"w\">    </span>__init__.py\n<span class=\"w\">    </span>phrases.py\n<span class=\"w\">    </span>board.py\nmain.py\n\n<span class=\"c1\"># in main.py</span>\nfrom<span class=\"w\"> </span>aac.phrases<span class=\"w\"> </span>import<span class=\"w\"> </span>load_phrases\nfrom<span class=\"w\"> </span>aac<span class=\"w\"> </span>import<span class=\"w\"> </span>board</code></pre></div><p>Java ties packages to folders and requires <code>package</code> lines. Python only needs the folder structure.</p>", "text": "aac/ __init__.py phrases.py board.py main.py # in main.py from aac.phrases import load_phrases from aac import board java ties packages to folders and requires package lines. python only needs the folder structure."}, {"id": "py-w7-venv", "title": "venv and pip", "src": "Tutorial 12", "idea": "A virtual environment gives each project its own Python and packages. pip installs packages from PyPI, a bit like Maven for Java.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"nb\">cd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>~/python-course\npython3<span class=\"w\"> </span>-m<span class=\"w\"> </span>venv<span class=\"w\"> </span>.venv<span class=\"w\">          </span><span class=\"c1\"># create once</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">source</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>.venv/bin/activate<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\"># each new terminal; prompt shows (.venv)</span>\npip<span class=\"w\"> </span>install<span class=\"w\"> </span>requests<span class=\"w\">           </span><span class=\"c1\"># installs into .venv only</span>\npip<span class=\"w\"> </span>freeze<span class=\"w\"> </span>&gt;<span class=\"w\"> </span>requirements.txt<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\"># record the versions</span>\ndeactivate<span class=\"w\">                     </span><span class=\"c1\"># leave the venv</span></code></pre></div><ul><li>Add <code>.venv/</code> to <code>.gitignore</code>. Commit <code>requirements.txt</code> instead.</li><li>On macOS with Homebrew Python, pip refuses to install outside a venv (\"externally-managed-environment\"). That's on purpose. Use a venv.</li><li>Only install packages you trust. Typo-named fake packages on PyPI are a real attack. <span class=\"by\">Added by Fern</span></li></ul>", "text": "cd ~/python-course python3 -m venv .venv # create once source .venv/bin/activate # each new terminal; prompt shows (.venv) pip install requests # installs into .venv only pip freeze > requirements.txt # record the versions deactivate # leave the venv add .venv/ to .gitignore . commit requirements.txt instead. on macos with homebrew python, pip refuses to install outside a venv (\"externally-managed-environment\"). that's on purpose. use a venv. only install packages you trust. typo-named fake packages on pypi are a real attack. added by fern"}, {"id": "py-w7-ex", "title": "Exercise: split the game into modules", "src": "Exercise", "idea": "Move the guessing game's helper functions into their own module and import them from a main file.", "deep": "<p><strong>Try it:</strong> Make <code>helpers.py</code> with <code>get_guess()</code> and <code>game.py</code> that imports it and runs the game.</p><details><summary>Hint 1 (nudge)</summary><p>Both files go in the same folder. The import uses the file name without .py.</p></details><details><summary>Hint 2 (stronger)</summary><p>In game.py: <code>from helpers import get_guess</code>, then put the game loop under <code>if __name__ == \"__main__\":</code>.</p></details><details><summary>Answer</summary><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"c1\"># helpers.py</span>\n<span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">get_guess</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">low</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">high</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">while</span> <span class=\"kc\">True</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n        <span class=\"n\">text</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"nb\">input</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Guess </span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">low</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">-</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">high</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">: \"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"n\">text</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">isdigit</span><span class=\"p\">()</span> <span class=\"ow\">and</span> <span class=\"n\">low</span> <span class=\"o\">&lt;=</span> <span class=\"nb\">int</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">text</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"o\">&lt;=</span> <span class=\"n\">high</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n            <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"nb\">int</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">text</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n        <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Please enter a number in range.\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\"># game.py</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">random</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">from</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">helpers</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">import</span> <span class=\"n\">get_guess</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">play</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">max_n</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"mi\">100</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n    <span class=\"o\">...</span>  <span class=\"c1\"># same as week 4</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"vm\">__name__</span> <span class=\"o\">==</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"__main__\"</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Got it in </span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">play</span><span class=\"p\">()</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\"> tries!\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span></code></pre></div></details><details><summary>🔁 Explain it back: what happens if game.py has no __main__ check and another file imports it?</summary><p>Remember that importing a module runs its top-level code.</p></details>", "text": "try it: make helpers.py with get_guess() and game.py that imports it and runs the game. hint 1 (nudge) both files go in the same folder. the import uses the file name without .py. hint 2 (stronger) in game.py: from helpers import get_guess , then put the game loop under if __name__ == \"__main__\": . answer # helpers.py def get_guess ( low , high ): while true : text = input ( f \"guess { low } - { high } : \" ) if text . isdigit () and low <= int ( text ) <= high : return int ( text ) print ( \"please enter a number in range.\" ) # game.py import random from helpers import get_guess def play ( max_n = 100 ): ... # same as week 4 if __name__ == \"__main__\" : print ( f \"got it in { play () } tries!\" ) 🔁 explain it back: what happens if game.py has no __main__ check and another file imports it? remember that importing a module runs its top-level code."}]}, {"id": "py-w8", "title": "Week 8 · Files, JSON and exceptions", "blurb": "Reading and writing files, the with block, JSON, and try/except. ~4 hrs.", "topics": [{"id": "py-w8-plan", "title": "This week's plan", "src": "Course plan", "idea": "Reading and writing files, the with block, JSON, and try/except.", "deep": "<p>About <strong>3.8 hours</strong> this week, in 6 sessions. Do one per sitting.</p><ol><li><strong>35 min:</strong> open() and with</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> Reading and writing text</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> The json module</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> try/except/else/finally</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> Raising your own exceptions</li><li><strong>45 min:</strong> Exercise + explain-it-back question</li></ol><p>Reading: <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/inputoutput.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tutorial ch. 7</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/errors.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tutorial ch. 8</a>, <a href=\"https://www.learnpython.org/en/Exception_Handling\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">learnpython.org: Exception Handling</a>.</p>", "text": "about 3.8 hours this week, in 6 sessions. do one per sitting. 35 min: open() and with 35 min: reading and writing text 35 min: the json module 40 min: try/except/else/finally 35 min: raising your own exceptions 45 min: exercise + explain-it-back question reading: tutorial ch. 7 , tutorial ch. 8 , learnpython.org: exception handling ."}, {"id": "py-w8-with", "title": "Files and the with block", "src": "Tutorial 7.2", "idea": "with open(path) as f: opens a file and closes it automatically, even after an error. It's Java's try-with-resources.", "deep": "<p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Python</strong></p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"k\">with</span> <span class=\"nb\">open</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"notes.txt\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"w\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"k\">as</span> <span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">write</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"first line</span><span class=\"se\">\\n</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">with</span> <span class=\"nb\">open</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"notes.txt\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"k\">as</span> <span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>      <span class=\"c1\"># \"r\" is the default</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">line</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n        <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">line</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">strip</span><span class=\"p\">())</span></code></pre></div><p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Java</strong> (what you already know)</p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"k\">try</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"kd\">var</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Files</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">newBufferedWriter</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Path</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">of</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"notes.txt\"</span><span class=\"p\">)))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">write</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"first line\\n\"</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"k\">try</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"kd\">var</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Files</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">newBufferedReader</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Path</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">of</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"notes.txt\"</span><span class=\"p\">)))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">lines</span><span class=\"p\">().</span><span class=\"na\">forEach</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">-&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">System</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">out</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">println</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">strip</span><span class=\"p\">()));</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span></code></pre></div><ul><li>Modes: <code>\"r\"</code> read, <code>\"w\"</code> write (erases the file first), <code>\"a\"</code> append.</li><li><code>f.read()</code> reads it all as one string. <code>f.readlines()</code> gives a list of lines.</li></ul>", "text": "python with open ( \"notes.txt\" , \"w\" ) as f : f . write ( \"first line \\n \" ) with open ( \"notes.txt\" ) as f : # \"r\" is the default for line in f : print ( line . strip ()) java (what you already know) try ( var w = files . newbufferedwriter ( path . of ( \"notes.txt\" ))) { w . write ( \"first line\\n\" ); } try ( var r = files . newbufferedreader ( path . of ( \"notes.txt\" ))) { r . lines (). foreach ( l -> system . out . println ( l . strip ())); } modes: \"r\" read, \"w\" write (erases the file first), \"a\" append. f.read() reads it all as one string. f.readlines() gives a list of lines."}, {"id": "py-w8-json", "title": "JSON", "src": "Tutorial 7.2.2", "idea": "The json module turns dicts and lists into JSON text and back. Your study site's data files are JSON too.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">json</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">scores</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">{</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Noah\"</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">12</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">9</span><span class=\"p\">],</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"Ana\"</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">7</span><span class=\"p\">]}</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">with</span> <span class=\"nb\">open</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"scores.json\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"w\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"k\">as</span> <span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">json</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">dump</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">scores</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">indent</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>     <span class=\"c1\"># write</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">with</span> <span class=\"nb\">open</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"scores.json\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"k\">as</span> <span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">loaded</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">json</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">load</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>              <span class=\"c1\"># read back into a dict</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">text</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">json</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">dumps</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">scores</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>              <span class=\"c1\"># to a string</span>\n<span class=\"n\">json</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">loads</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">text</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>                       <span class=\"c1\"># from a string</span></code></pre></div><p>No library needed, unlike Java where you'd add Jackson or Gson.</p>", "text": "import json scores = { \"noah\" : [ 12 , 9 ], \"ana\" : [ 7 ]} with open ( \"scores.json\" , \"w\" ) as f : json . dump ( scores , f , indent = 2 ) # write with open ( \"scores.json\" ) as f : loaded = json . load ( f ) # read back into a dict text = json . dumps ( scores ) # to a string json . loads ( text ) # from a string no library needed, unlike java where you'd add jackson or gson."}, {"id": "py-w8-try", "title": "try / except / else / finally", "src": "Tutorial 8.3", "idea": "except replaces catch. else runs only if nothing was raised. finally always runs. Python has no checked exceptions, so nothing forces you to handle one.", "deep": "<p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Python</strong></p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"k\">try</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">n</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"nb\">int</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nb\">input</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Number? \"</span><span class=\"p\">))</span>\n<span class=\"k\">except</span> <span class=\"ne\">ValueError</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Not a number\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">except</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"ne\">KeyError</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"ne\">IndexError</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"k\">as</span> <span class=\"n\">e</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Lookup failed:\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">e</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">else</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"You typed\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">finally</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"done\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span></code></pre></div><p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Java</strong> (what you already know)</p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"k\">try</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"kt\">int</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Integer</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">parseInt</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">sc</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">nextLine</span><span class=\"p\">());</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">System</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">out</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">println</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"You typed \"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">catch</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">NumberFormatException</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">System</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">out</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">println</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"Not a number\"</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">finally</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">System</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">out</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">println</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"done\"</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span></code></pre></div><p>Avoid a bare <code>except:</code>. It catches everything, even Ctrl+C. Catch the specific error you expect.</p>", "text": "python try : n = int ( input ( \"number? \" )) except valueerror : print ( \"not a number\" ) except ( keyerror , indexerror ) as e : print ( \"lookup failed:\" , e ) else : print ( \"you typed\" , n ) finally : print ( \"done\" ) java (what you already know) try { int n = integer . parseint ( sc . nextline ()); system . out . println ( \"you typed \" + n ); } catch ( numberformatexception e ) { system . out . println ( \"not a number\" ); } finally { system . out . println ( \"done\" ); } avoid a bare except: . it catches everything, even ctrl+c. catch the specific error you expect."}, {"id": "py-w8-raise", "title": "Raising your own exceptions", "src": "Tutorial 8.4–8.6", "idea": "raise replaces throw. Make your own exception type by subclassing Exception.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"k\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">InvalidPhraseError</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"ne\">Exception</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">pass</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">add_phrase</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">board</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">text</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"ow\">not</span> <span class=\"n\">text</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">strip</span><span class=\"p\">():</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">raise</span> <span class=\"n\">InvalidPhraseError</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Phrase can't be empty\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">board</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">append</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">text</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">try</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">add_phrase</span><span class=\"p\">([],</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"   \"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">except</span> <span class=\"n\">InvalidPhraseError</span> <span class=\"k\">as</span> <span class=\"n\">e</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">e</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>          <span class=\"c1\"># Phrase can't be empty</span></code></pre></div><p>Common built-ins: <code>ValueError</code> (right type, bad value), <code>TypeError</code>, <code>KeyError</code>, <code>IndexError</code>, <code>FileNotFoundError</code>.</p>", "text": "class invalidphraseerror ( exception ): pass def add_phrase ( board , text ): if not text . strip (): raise invalidphraseerror ( \"phrase can't be empty\" ) board . append ( text ) try : add_phrase ([], \" \" ) except invalidphraseerror as e : print ( e ) # phrase can't be empty common built-ins: valueerror (right type, bad value), typeerror , keyerror , indexerror , filenotfounderror ."}, {"id": "py-w8-ex", "title": "Exercise: save high scores", "src": "Exercise", "idea": "Save the guessing game's best score per player to a JSON file, and load it when the game starts. If the file doesn't exist yet, start empty.", "deep": "<p><strong>Try it:</strong> Write <code>load_scores()</code> and <code>save_scores(scores)</code> for <code>scores.json</code>.</p><details><summary>Hint 1 (nudge)</summary><p>The first run has no file. Which exception does open() raise then?</p></details><details><summary>Hint 2 (stronger)</summary><p>Wrap the load in <code>try</code> and catch <code>FileNotFoundError</code>, returning <code>{}</code>. Keep the lower score with <code>min()</code>.</p></details><details><summary>Answer</summary><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">json</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">load_scores</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">path</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"scores.json\"</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">try</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">with</span> <span class=\"nb\">open</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">path</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"k\">as</span> <span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n            <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"n\">json</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">load</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">except</span> <span class=\"ne\">FileNotFoundError</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"p\">{}</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">save_scores</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">scores</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">path</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"scores.json\"</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">with</span> <span class=\"nb\">open</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">path</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"w\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"k\">as</span> <span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n        <span class=\"n\">json</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">dump</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">scores</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">indent</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">scores</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">load_scores</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>\n<span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">tries</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"Noah\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">7</span>\n<span class=\"n\">scores</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">]</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"nb\">min</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">tries</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">scores</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">get</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">tries</span><span class=\"p\">))</span>\n<span class=\"n\">save_scores</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">scores</span><span class=\"p\">)</span></code></pre></div></details><details><summary>🔁 Explain it back: why is with open(...) safer than calling f.close() yourself?</summary><p>Think about what happens to the close() line if an error is raised halfway through.</p></details>", "text": "try it: write load_scores() and save_scores(scores) for scores.json . hint 1 (nudge) the first run has no file. which exception does open() raise then? hint 2 (stronger) wrap the load in try and catch filenotfounderror , returning {} . keep the lower score with min() . answer import json def load_scores ( path = \"scores.json\" ): try : with open ( path ) as f : return json . load ( f ) except filenotfounderror : return {} def save_scores ( scores , path = \"scores.json\" ): with open ( path , \"w\" ) as f : json . dump ( scores , f , indent = 2 ) scores = load_scores () name , tries = \"noah\" , 7 scores [ name ] = min ( tries , scores . get ( name , tries )) save_scores ( scores ) 🔁 explain it back: why is with open(...) safer than calling f.close() yourself? think about what happens to the close() line if an error is raised halfway through."}]}, {"id": "py-w9", "title": "Week 9 · Classes and objects", "blurb": "self, __init__, inheritance, dunder methods and dataclasses. Lots of overlap with Java. ~4 hrs.", "topics": [{"id": "py-w9-plan", "title": "This week's plan", "src": "Course plan", "idea": "self, __init__, inheritance, dunder methods and dataclasses. Lots of overlap with Java.", "deep": "<p>About <strong>3.8 hours</strong> this week, in 6 sessions. Do one per sitting.</p><ol><li><strong>40 min:</strong> class, __init__ and self</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> Public by default</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> Inheritance and super()</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> Dunder methods</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> @dataclass</li><li><strong>45 min:</strong> Exercise + explain-it-back question</li></ol><p>Reading: <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/classes.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tutorial ch. 9</a>, <a href=\"https://www.learnpython.org/en/Classes_and_Objects\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">learnpython.org: Classes and Objects</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/library/dataclasses.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">dataclasses docs</a>.</p>", "text": "about 3.8 hours this week, in 6 sessions. do one per sitting. 40 min: class, __init__ and self 35 min: public by default 40 min: inheritance and super() 35 min: dunder methods 35 min: @dataclass 45 min: exercise + explain-it-back question reading: tutorial ch. 9 , learnpython.org: classes and objects , dataclasses docs ."}, {"id": "py-w9-class", "title": "Classes, __init__ and self", "src": "Tutorial 9.3", "idea": "__init__ is the constructor. Every method takes self first, which is Java's this, but written out. Fields are created by assigning to self.", "deep": "<p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Python</strong></p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"k\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">Phrase</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"fm\">__init__</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">text</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">category</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"general\"</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n        <span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">text</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">text</span>\n        <span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">category</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">category</span>\n        <span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">uses</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span>\n\n    <span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">speak</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n        <span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">uses</span> <span class=\"o\">+=</span> <span class=\"mi\">1</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">text</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">p</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">Phrase</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"I'm hungry\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"needs\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>   <span class=\"c1\"># no 'new'</span>\n<span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">speak</span><span class=\"p\">()</span></code></pre></div><p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Java</strong> (what you already know)</p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kd\">public</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kd\">class</span> <span class=\"nc\">Phrase</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"kd\">private</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">text</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">category</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"kd\">private</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">int</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kd\">uses</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"kd\">public</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">Phrase</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">text</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">category</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"k\">this</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">text</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">text</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"k\">this</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">category</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">category</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"kd\">public</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">speak</span><span class=\"p\">()</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"kd\">uses</span><span class=\"o\">++</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"k\">return</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">text</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"n\">Phrase</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">new</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Phrase</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"I'm hungry\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s\">\"needs\"</span><span class=\"p\">);</span></code></pre></div><p>No field declarations, no <code>new</code>, and one <code>__init__</code> with default values instead of several constructors.</p>", "text": "python class phrase : def __init__ ( self , text , category = \"general\" ): self . text = text self . category = category self . uses = 0 def speak ( self ): self . uses += 1 return self . text p = phrase ( \"i'm hungry\" , \"needs\" ) # no 'new' p . speak () java (what you already know) public class phrase { private string text , category ; private int uses = 0 ; public phrase ( string text , string category ) { this . text = text ; this . category = category ; } public string speak () { uses ++ ; return text ; } } phrase p = new phrase ( \"i'm hungry\" , \"needs\" ); no field declarations, no new , and one __init__ with default values instead of several constructors."}, {"id": "py-w9-private", "title": "No private: conventions instead", "src": "Tutorial 9.6", "idea": "Python has no private keyword. A leading _underscore means \"internal, please don't touch\". @property gives you getters without getX() methods.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"k\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">Account</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"fm\">__init__</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">balance</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n        <span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">_balance</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">balance</span>      <span class=\"c1\"># 'private' by convention</span>\n\n    <span class=\"nd\">@property</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">balance</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>               <span class=\"c1\"># read like a field: acct.balance</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">_balance</span>\n\n    <span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">deposit</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">amount</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"n\">amount</span> <span class=\"o\">&lt;=</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n            <span class=\"k\">raise</span> <span class=\"ne\">ValueError</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Deposit must be positive\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n        <span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">_balance</span> <span class=\"o\">+=</span> <span class=\"n\">amount</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">acct</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">Account</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">50</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">acct</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">balance</span>          <span class=\"c1\"># 50, no parentheses</span>\n<span class=\"n\">acct</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">balance</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"mi\">10</span>     <span class=\"c1\"># AttributeError: no setter defined</span></code></pre></div>", "text": "class account : def __init__ ( self , balance ): self . _balance = balance # 'private' by convention @property def balance ( self ): # read like a field: acct.balance return self . _balance def deposit ( self , amount ): if amount <= 0 : raise valueerror ( \"deposit must be positive\" ) self . _balance += amount acct = account ( 50 ) acct . balance # 50, no parentheses acct . balance = 10 # attributeerror: no setter defined"}, {"id": "py-w9-inherit", "title": "Inheritance and super()", "src": "Tutorial 9.5", "idea": "Put the parent class in parentheses. super().__init__() calls the parent constructor. Every method can be overridden, with no @Override needed.", "deep": "<p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Python</strong></p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"k\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">Animal</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"fm\">__init__</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n        <span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">name</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">name</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">sound</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"...\"</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">Dog</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Animal</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"fm\">__init__</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">breed</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n        <span class=\"nb\">super</span><span class=\"p\">()</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"fm\">__init__</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n        <span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">breed</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">breed</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">sound</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"Woof\"</span>\n\n<span class=\"nb\">isinstance</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Dog</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Rex\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"lab\"</span><span class=\"p\">),</span> <span class=\"n\">Animal</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>   <span class=\"c1\"># True</span></code></pre></div><p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Java</strong> (what you already know)</p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kd\">class</span> <span class=\"nc\">Dog</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kd\">extends</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Animal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"kd\">private</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">breed</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">Dog</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">breed</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"kd\">super</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"k\">this</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">breed</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">breed</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nd\">@Override</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">sound</span><span class=\"p\">()</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">return</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s\">\"Woof\"</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">// instanceof</span></code></pre></div><p>Python has no <code>interface</code> keyword. It relies on \"duck typing\": if an object has a <code>sound()</code> method, you can call it. <code>abc.ABC</code> exists for abstract classes when you need them.</p>", "text": "python class animal : def __init__ ( self , name ): self . name = name def sound ( self ): return \"...\" class dog ( animal ): def __init__ ( self , name , breed ): super () . __init__ ( name ) self . breed = breed def sound ( self ): return \"woof\" isinstance ( dog ( \"rex\" , \"lab\" ), animal ) # true java (what you already know) class dog extends animal { private string breed ; dog ( string name , string breed ) { super ( name ); this . breed = breed ; } @override string sound () { return \"woof\" ; } } // instanceof python has no interface keyword. it relies on \"duck typing\": if an object has a sound() method, you can call it. abc.abc exists for abstract classes when you need them."}, {"id": "py-w9-dunder", "title": "Dunder methods", "src": "Data model docs", "idea": "Methods named __like_this__ hook into Python's built-in behavior: __str__ is toString(), __eq__ is equals(), __len__ makes len() work.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"k\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">Board</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"fm\">__init__</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">phrases</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n        <span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">name</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">name</span>\n        <span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">phrases</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">phrases</span>\n\n    <span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"fm\">__str__</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>              <span class=\"c1\"># print(board), str(board)</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\"> (</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"nb\">len</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\"> phrases)\"</span>\n\n    <span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"fm\">__repr__</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>             <span class=\"c1\"># what the REPL shows</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Board(</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"si\">!r}</span><span class=\"s2\">, </span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">phrases</span><span class=\"si\">!r}</span><span class=\"s2\">)\"</span>\n\n    <span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"fm\">__len__</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>              <span class=\"c1\"># len(board)</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"nb\">len</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">phrases</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n    <span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"fm\">__eq__</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">other</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>        <span class=\"c1\"># board1 == board2</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"nb\">isinstance</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">other</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">Board</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"ow\">and</span> <span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">phrases</span> <span class=\"o\">==</span> <span class=\"n\">other</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">phrases</span></code></pre></div><p>In Python, <code>==</code> calls <code>__eq__</code> (like <code>.equals()</code>). <code>is</code> checks if two names are the same object (like Java's <code>==</code> on objects).</p>", "text": "class board : def __init__ ( self , name , phrases ): self . name = name self . phrases = phrases def __str__ ( self ): # print(board), str(board) return f \" { self . name } ( { len ( self ) } phrases)\" def __repr__ ( self ): # what the repl shows return f \"board( { self . name !r} , { self . phrases !r} )\" def __len__ ( self ): # len(board) return len ( self . phrases ) def __eq__ ( self , other ): # board1 == board2 return isinstance ( other , board ) and self . phrases == other . phrases in python, == calls __eq__ (like .equals() ). is checks if two names are the same object (like java's == on objects)."}, {"id": "py-w9-dataclass", "title": "@dataclass", "src": "dataclasses docs", "idea": "@dataclass writes __init__, __repr__ and __eq__ for you from typed fields. It's Python's answer to Java records.", "deep": "<p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Python</strong></p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kn\">from</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">dataclasses</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">import</span> <span class=\"n\">dataclass</span>\n\n<span class=\"nd\">@dataclass</span>\n<span class=\"k\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">Phrase</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">text</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"nb\">str</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">category</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"nb\">str</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"general\"</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">uses</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"nb\">int</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">Phrase</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Yes\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>    <span class=\"c1\"># Phrase(text='Yes', category='general', uses=0)</span></code></pre></div><p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Java</strong> (what you already know)</p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kd\">public</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kd\">record</span> <span class=\"nc\">Phrase</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">text</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">String</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">category</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">int</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kd\">uses</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{}</span></code></pre></div><p>The <code>: str</code> parts are <strong>type hints</strong>. Python doesn't enforce them while running, but your IDE and tools like mypy check them.</p>", "text": "python from dataclasses import dataclass @dataclass class phrase : text : str category : str = \"general\" uses : int = 0 phrase ( \"yes\" ) # phrase(text='yes', category='general', uses=0) java (what you already know) public record phrase ( string text , string category , int uses ) {} the : str parts are type hints . python doesn't enforce them while running, but your ide and tools like mypy check them."}, {"id": "py-w9-ex", "title": "Exercise: AAC board model", "src": "Exercise", "idea": "Model part of your AAC app: a Phrase dataclass and a Board class that can add phrases, list them by category, and print nicely.", "deep": "<p><strong>Try it:</strong> Make <code>Board.add(phrase)</code>, <code>Board.by_category(cat)</code> and a <code>__str__</code>.</p><details><summary>Hint 1 (nudge)</summary><p>Board needs a name and a list of Phrase objects. by_category is a filter over that list.</p></details><details><summary>Hint 2 (stronger)</summary><p>Store <code>self.phrases = []</code> in <code>__init__</code>. Use a list comprehension in <code>by_category</code>.</p></details><details><summary>Answer</summary><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kn\">from</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">dataclasses</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">import</span> <span class=\"n\">dataclass</span>\n\n<span class=\"nd\">@dataclass</span>\n<span class=\"k\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">Phrase</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">text</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"nb\">str</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">category</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"nb\">str</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"general\"</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">Board</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"fm\">__init__</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n        <span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">name</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">name</span>\n        <span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">phrases</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">[]</span>\n\n    <span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">add</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">phrase</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n        <span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">phrases</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">append</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">phrase</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n    <span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">by_category</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">cat</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"n\">p</span> <span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">p</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">phrases</span> <span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">category</span> <span class=\"o\">==</span> <span class=\"n\">cat</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>\n\n    <span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"fm\">__str__</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">: </span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"nb\">len</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">phrases</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\"> phrases\"</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">b</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">Board</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Home\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">add</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Phrase</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"I'm hungry\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"needs\"</span><span class=\"p\">))</span>\n<span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">add</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Phrase</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Hi!\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"social\"</span><span class=\"p\">))</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">by_category</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"needs\"</span><span class=\"p\">))</span></code></pre></div></details><details><summary>🔁 Explain it back: what's the difference between == and is in Python?</summary><p>Map each one to its Java match: which compares contents and which compares references?</p></details>", "text": "try it: make board.add(phrase) , board.by_category(cat) and a __str__ . hint 1 (nudge) board needs a name and a list of phrase objects. by_category is a filter over that list. hint 2 (stronger) store self.phrases = [] in __init__ . use a list comprehension in by_category . answer from dataclasses import dataclass @dataclass class phrase : text : str category : str = \"general\" class board : def __init__ ( self , name ): self . name = name self . phrases = [] def add ( self , phrase ): self . phrases . append ( phrase ) def by_category ( self , cat ): return [ p for p in self . phrases if p . category == cat ] def __str__ ( self ): return f \" { self . name } : { len ( self . phrases ) } phrases\" b = board ( \"home\" ) b . add ( phrase ( \"i'm hungry\" , \"needs\" )) b . add ( phrase ( \"hi!\" , \"social\" )) print ( b , b . by_category ( \"needs\" )) 🔁 explain it back: what's the difference between == and is in python? map each one to its java match: which compares contents and which compares references?"}]}, {"id": "py-w10", "title": "Week 10 · Standard library tour", "blurb": "pathlib, datetime, collections, re, random and math. ~4 hrs.", "topics": [{"id": "py-w10-plan", "title": "This week's plan", "src": "Course plan", "idea": "pathlib, datetime, collections, re, random and math.", "deep": "<p>About <strong>3.7 hours</strong> this week, in 6 sessions. Do one per sitting.</p><ol><li><strong>35 min:</strong> pathlib and os</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> datetime</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> collections</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> re (regular expressions)</li><li><strong>30 min:</strong> random, math, statistics</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> Exercise + explain-it-back question</li></ol><p>Reading: <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/stdlib.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tutorial ch. 10</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/stdlib2.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tutorial ch. 11</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/library/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Library Reference</a>.</p>", "text": "about 3.7 hours this week, in 6 sessions. do one per sitting. 35 min: pathlib and os 35 min: datetime 40 min: collections 40 min: re (regular expressions) 30 min: random, math, statistics 40 min: exercise + explain-it-back question reading: tutorial ch. 10 , tutorial ch. 11 , library reference ."}, {"id": "py-w10-path", "title": "pathlib: files and folders", "src": "Library: pathlib", "idea": "Path objects handle file paths on any OS. The / operator joins paths. Similar to java.nio.file.Path.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kn\">from</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">pathlib</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">import</span> <span class=\"n\">Path</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">home</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">Path</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">home</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>                       <span class=\"c1\"># /Users/noah</span>\n<span class=\"n\">notes</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">home</span> <span class=\"o\">/</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"fern-study-notes\"</span>\n<span class=\"n\">notes</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">exists</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>\n<span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">f</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">notes</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">glob</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"*.js\"</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>             <span class=\"c1\"># every .js file</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">stat</span><span class=\"p\">()</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">st_size</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">p</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">Path</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"data/python.js\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">suffix</span>      <span class=\"c1\"># '.js'</span>\n<span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">stem</span>        <span class=\"c1\"># 'python'</span>\n<span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">read_text</span><span class=\"p\">()</span> <span class=\"c1\"># whole file as a string</span></code></pre></div>", "text": "from pathlib import path home = path . home () # /users/noah notes = home / \"fern-study-notes\" notes . exists () for f in notes . glob ( \"*.js\" ): # every .js file print ( f . name , f . stat () . st_size ) p = path ( \"data/python.js\" ) p . suffix # '.js' p . stem # 'python' p . read_text () # whole file as a string"}, {"id": "py-w10-dt", "title": "datetime", "src": "Library: datetime", "idea": "date, datetime and timedelta handle dates and time math. strftime formats them, like Java's DateTimeFormatter.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kn\">from</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">datetime</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">import</span> <span class=\"n\">date</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">datetime</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">timedelta</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">today</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">date</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">today</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>\n<span class=\"n\">due</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">today</span> <span class=\"o\">+</span> <span class=\"n\">timedelta</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">weeks</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"mi\">12</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">due</span> <span class=\"o\">-</span> <span class=\"n\">today</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">days</span>                     <span class=\"c1\"># 84</span>\n<span class=\"n\">datetime</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">now</span><span class=\"p\">()</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">strftime</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"%Y-%m-</span><span class=\"si\">%d</span><span class=\"s2\"> %H:%M\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">datetime</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">strptime</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"2026-10-29\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"%Y-%m-</span><span class=\"si\">%d</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>   <span class=\"c1\"># parse</span></code></pre></div>", "text": "from datetime import date , datetime , timedelta today = date . today () due = today + timedelta ( weeks = 12 ) ( due - today ) . days # 84 datetime . now () . strftime ( \"%y-%m- %d %h:%m\" ) datetime . strptime ( \"2026-10-29\" , \"%y-%m- %d \" ) # parse"}, {"id": "py-w10-coll", "title": "collections", "src": "Library: collections", "idea": "Counter counts things, defaultdict gives missing keys a default, and deque is a fast queue from both ends.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kn\">from</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">collections</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">import</span> <span class=\"n\">Counter</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">defaultdict</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">deque</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">Counter</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"mississippi\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">most_common</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>   <span class=\"c1\"># [('i', 4), ('s', 4)]</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">groups</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">defaultdict</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nb\">list</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">word</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"s2\">\"fern\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"fig\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"moss\"</span><span class=\"p\">]:</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">groups</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"n\">word</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">]]</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">append</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">word</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>        <span class=\"c1\"># no KeyError</span>\n<span class=\"c1\"># {'f': ['fern', 'fig'], 'm': ['moss']}</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">q</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">deque</span><span class=\"p\">([</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">])</span>\n<span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">appendleft</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">);</span> <span class=\"n\">q</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">pop</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>                <span class=\"c1\"># like Java's ArrayDeque</span></code></pre></div>", "text": "from collections import counter , defaultdict , deque counter ( \"mississippi\" ) . most_common ( 2 ) # [('i', 4), ('s', 4)] groups = defaultdict ( list ) for word in [ \"fern\" , \"fig\" , \"moss\" ]: groups [ word [ 0 ]] . append ( word ) # no keyerror # {'f': ['fern', 'fig'], 'm': ['moss']} q = deque ([ 1 , 2 , 3 ]) q . appendleft ( 0 ); q . pop () # like java's arraydeque"}, {"id": "py-w10-re", "title": "re: regular expressions", "src": "Regex HOWTO", "idea": "The re module searches text with patterns. Write patterns as raw strings, r\"...\", so backslashes stay as-is.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">re</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">text</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"Email ana@umgc.edu or ben@example.com\"</span>\n<span class=\"n\">re</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">findall</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"sa\">r</span><span class=\"s2\">\"[\\w.]+@[\\w.]+\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">text</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"c1\"># ['ana@umgc.edu', 'ben@example.com']</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">m</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">re</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">search</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"sa\">r</span><span class=\"s2\">\"(\\d</span><span class=\"si\">{4}</span><span class=\"s2\">)-(\\d</span><span class=\"si\">{2}</span><span class=\"s2\">)\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"Due 2027-01\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">group</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>                    <span class=\"c1\"># '2027'</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">re</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">sub</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"sa\">r</span><span class=\"s2\">\"\\s+\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\" \"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"too    many   spaces\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span></code></pre></div><p>Useful for log files in security work. Practice patterns at the <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/howto/regex.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Regex HOWTO</a>.</p>", "text": "import re text = \"email ana@umgc.edu or ben@example.com\" re . findall ( r \"[\\w.]+@[\\w.]+\" , text ) # ['ana@umgc.edu', 'ben@example.com'] m = re . search ( r \"(\\d {4} )-(\\d {2} )\" , \"due 2027-01\" ) m . group ( 1 ) # '2027' re . sub ( r \"\\s+\" , \" \" , \"too many spaces\" ) useful for log files in security work. practice patterns at the regex howto ."}, {"id": "py-w10-misc", "title": "random, math and statistics", "src": "Tutorial 10.6–10.7", "idea": "Small modules you'll use all the time.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">random</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">math</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">statistics</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">random</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">choice</span><span class=\"p\">([</span><span class=\"s2\">\"rock\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"paper\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"scissors\"</span><span class=\"p\">])</span>\n<span class=\"n\">random</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">shuffle</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">cards</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>                  <span class=\"c1\"># in place</span>\n<span class=\"n\">math</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">floor</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mf\">2.7</span><span class=\"p\">),</span> <span class=\"n\">math</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ceil</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mf\">2.1</span><span class=\"p\">),</span> <span class=\"n\">math</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">pi</span>\n<span class=\"n\">statistics</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mean</span><span class=\"p\">([</span><span class=\"mi\">88</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">52</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">95</span><span class=\"p\">])</span>\n<span class=\"n\">statistics</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">median</span><span class=\"p\">([</span><span class=\"mi\">88</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">52</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">95</span><span class=\"p\">])</span></code></pre></div><p><code>random</code> is fine for games, but never for passwords or tokens. Use <code>secrets</code> for anything security-related. <span class=\"by\">Added by Fern</span></p>", "text": "import random , math , statistics random . choice ([ \"rock\" , \"paper\" , \"scissors\" ]) random . shuffle ( cards ) # in place math . floor ( 2.7 ), math . ceil ( 2.1 ), math . pi statistics . mean ([ 88 , 52 , 95 ]) statistics . median ([ 88 , 52 , 95 ]) random is fine for games, but never for passwords or tokens. use secrets for anything security-related. added by fern"}, {"id": "py-w10-ex", "title": "Exercise: file organizer", "src": "Exercise", "idea": "Write a script that counts the files in a folder by extension and prints a small report. Don't move or delete anything.", "deep": "<p><strong>Try it:</strong> Report on your <code>~/Downloads</code> folder, for example <code>.pdf: 12</code>.</p><details><summary>Hint 1 (nudge)</summary><p>You need to list the files in a folder, get each extension, and count the extensions.</p></details><details><summary>Hint 2 (stronger)</summary><p>Combine <code>Path.home() / \"Downloads\"</code>, <code>.iterdir()</code>, <code>.is_file()</code>, <code>.suffix</code> and a <code>Counter</code>.</p></details><details><summary>Answer</summary><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kn\">from</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">pathlib</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">import</span> <span class=\"n\">Path</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">from</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">collections</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">import</span> <span class=\"n\">Counter</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">folder</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">Path</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">home</span><span class=\"p\">()</span> <span class=\"o\">/</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"Downloads\"</span>\n<span class=\"n\">counts</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">Counter</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">suffix</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">lower</span><span class=\"p\">()</span> <span class=\"ow\">or</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"(none)\"</span>\n                 <span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">p</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">folder</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">iterdir</span><span class=\"p\">()</span> <span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">is_file</span><span class=\"p\">())</span>\n<span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">ext</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">n</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">counts</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">most_common</span><span class=\"p\">():</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">ext</span><span class=\"si\">:</span><span class=\"s2\">&gt;8</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">: </span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span></code></pre></div></details><details><summary>🔁 Explain it back: why use secrets instead of random for a password generator?</summary><p>Think about whether someone could predict the next \"random\" number.</p></details>", "text": "try it: report on your ~/downloads folder, for example .pdf: 12 . hint 1 (nudge) you need to list the files in a folder, get each extension, and count the extensions. hint 2 (stronger) combine path.home() / \"downloads\" , .iterdir() , .is_file() , .suffix and a counter . answer from pathlib import path from collections import counter folder = path . home () / \"downloads\" counts = counter ( p . suffix . lower () or \"(none)\" for p in folder . iterdir () if p . is_file ()) for ext , n in counts . most_common (): print ( f \" { ext : >8 } : { n } \" ) 🔁 explain it back: why use secrets instead of random for a password generator? think about whether someone could predict the next \"random\" number."}]}, {"id": "py-w11", "title": "Week 11 · Testing, debugging and security scripts", "blurb": "pytest, the debugger, and beginner security tools that only touch your own machine. ~4 hrs.", "topics": [{"id": "py-w11-plan", "title": "This week's plan", "src": "Course plan", "idea": "pytest, the debugger, and beginner security tools that only touch your own machine.", "deep": "<p>About <strong>3.8 hours</strong> this week, in 6 sessions. Do one per sitting.</p><ol><li><strong>40 min:</strong> pytest basics</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> Debugging with breakpoint()</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> hashlib: file hashes</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> socket: a localhost port checker</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> Ethics and scope</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> Exercise + explain-it-back question</li></ol><p>Reading: <a href=\"https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/getting-started.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pytest: Get started</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/library/pdb.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pdb docs</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hashlib docs</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/howto/sockets.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">socket HOWTO</a>.</p>", "text": "about 3.8 hours this week, in 6 sessions. do one per sitting. 40 min: pytest basics 35 min: debugging with breakpoint() 35 min: hashlib: file hashes 40 min: socket: a localhost port checker 35 min: ethics and scope 40 min: exercise + explain-it-back question reading: pytest: get started , pdb docs , hashlib docs , socket howto ."}, {"id": "py-w11-pytest", "title": "pytest: testing made short", "src": "pytest docs", "idea": "pytest finds files named test_*.py and runs every function starting with test_. A plain assert is the whole assertion API.", "deep": "<p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Python</strong></p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"c1\"># test_helpers.py</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">pytest</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">from</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">helpers</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">import</span> <span class=\"n\">is_valid_guess</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">test_in_range</span><span class=\"p\">():</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">assert</span> <span class=\"n\">is_valid_guess</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"50\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">100</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">test_not_a_number</span><span class=\"p\">():</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">assert</span> <span class=\"ow\">not</span> <span class=\"n\">is_valid_guess</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"abc\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">100</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">test_raises</span><span class=\"p\">():</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">with</span> <span class=\"n\">pytest</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">raises</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"ne\">ValueError</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n        <span class=\"nb\">int</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"abc\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span></code></pre></div><p class=\"vs-label\"><strong>Java</strong> (what you already know)</p><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kd\">class</span> <span class=\"nc\">HelpersTest</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nd\">@Test</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"kt\">void</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">inRange</span><span class=\"p\">()</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"n\">assertTrue</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Helpers</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">isValidGuess</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"50\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">100</span><span class=\"p\">));</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nd\">@Test</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"kt\">void</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">notANumber</span><span class=\"p\">()</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"n\">assertFalse</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Helpers</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"na\">isValidGuess</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"abc\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">100</span><span class=\"p\">));</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span></code></pre></div><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code>pip<span class=\"w\"> </span>install<span class=\"w\"> </span>pytest<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"c1\"># inside your venv</span>\npytest<span class=\"w\">                  </span><span class=\"c1\"># runs all tests</span>\npytest<span class=\"w\"> </span>-v<span class=\"w\">               </span><span class=\"c1\"># one line per test</span></code></pre></div><p>Same ideas as your CS 307 testing notes: unit tests, edge cases, regression tests.</p>", "text": "python # test_helpers.py import pytest from helpers import is_valid_guess def test_in_range (): assert is_valid_guess ( \"50\" , 1 , 100 ) def test_not_a_number (): assert not is_valid_guess ( \"abc\" , 1 , 100 ) def test_raises (): with pytest . raises ( valueerror ): int ( \"abc\" ) java (what you already know) class helperstest { @test void inrange () { asserttrue ( helpers . isvalidguess ( \"50\" , 1 , 100 )); } @test void notanumber () { assertfalse ( helpers . isvalidguess ( \"abc\" , 1 , 100 )); } } pip install pytest # inside your venv pytest # runs all tests pytest -v # one line per test same ideas as your cs 307 testing notes: unit tests, edge cases, regression tests."}, {"id": "py-w11-debug", "title": "Debugging", "src": "Library: pdb", "idea": "Put breakpoint() in your code to pause there and inspect variables. Or use your IDE's debugger, which works like IntelliJ's.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">average</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">total</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"nb\">sum</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">breakpoint</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>          <span class=\"c1\"># pauses here in the terminal</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"n\">total</span> <span class=\"o\">/</span> <span class=\"nb\">len</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\"># at the (Pdb) prompt:</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">#   p total      print a variable</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">#   n            next line</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">#   c            continue</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">#   q            quit</span></code></pre></div><p>Read tracebacks from the bottom up. The last line names the error, and the line above it shows where it happened.</p>", "text": "def average ( nums ): total = sum ( nums ) breakpoint () # pauses here in the terminal return total / len ( nums ) # at the (pdb) prompt: # p total print a variable # n next line # c continue # q quit read tracebacks from the bottom up. the last line names the error, and the line above it shows where it happened."}, {"id": "py-w11-hash", "title": "hashlib: check a file's fingerprint", "src": "Library: hashlib", "idea": "A hash is a fixed-size fingerprint of data. Compare a download's SHA-256 with the one the website publishes to make sure it wasn't changed.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">hashlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">sha256_of</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">path</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">h</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">hashlib</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">sha256</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">with</span> <span class=\"nb\">open</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">path</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"rb\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"k\">as</span> <span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>                 <span class=\"c1\"># \"rb\" = read bytes</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">chunk</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"nb\">iter</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"k\">lambda</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">read</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">8192</span><span class=\"p\">),</span> <span class=\"sa\">b</span><span class=\"s2\">\"\"</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n            <span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">update</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">chunk</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">hexdigest</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>\n\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">sha256_of</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"python-3.13.pkg\"</span><span class=\"p\">))</span></code></pre></div><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code>shasum<span class=\"w\"> </span>-a<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"m\">256</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>python-3.13.pkg<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"c1\"># same result from the terminal</span></code></pre></div><p>Hashing is not encryption: you can't get the file back from the hash. For storing passwords, use a slow, salted hash like <code>hashlib.scrypt</code>, never plain SHA-256.</p>", "text": "import hashlib def sha256_of ( path ): h = hashlib . sha256 () with open ( path , \"rb\" ) as f : # \"rb\" = read bytes for chunk in iter ( lambda : f . read ( 8192 ), b \"\" ): h . update ( chunk ) return h . hexdigest () print ( sha256_of ( \"python-3.13.pkg\" )) shasum -a 256 python-3.13.pkg # same result from the terminal hashing is not encryption: you can't get the file back from the hash. for storing passwords, use a slow, salted hash like hashlib.scrypt , never plain sha-256."}, {"id": "py-w11-socket", "title": "socket: a localhost port checker", "src": "Socket HOWTO", "idea": "A socket is one end of a network connection. Trying to connect to a port tells you if something is listening there. This version only checks your own Mac.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">socket</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">is_open</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">port</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">host</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"127.0.0.1\"</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">with</span> <span class=\"n\">socket</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">socket</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">socket</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">AF_INET</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">socket</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">SOCK_STREAM</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"k\">as</span> <span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n        <span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">settimeout</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mf\">0.5</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">connect_ex</span><span class=\"p\">((</span><span class=\"n\">host</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">port</span><span class=\"p\">))</span> <span class=\"o\">==</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span>   <span class=\"c1\"># 0 means connected</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">port</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">22</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">80</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">443</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">3000</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">5000</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">8080</span><span class=\"p\">]:</span>\n    <span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">port</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"open\"</span> <span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"n\">is_open</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">port</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"k\">else</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"closed\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span></code></pre></div><p>Start <code>python3 -m http.server 8080</code> in another terminal, run the checker, and watch 8080 flip to open.</p>", "text": "import socket def is_open ( port , host = \"127.0.0.1\" ): with socket . socket ( socket . af_inet , socket . sock_stream ) as s : s . settimeout ( 0.5 ) return s . connect_ex (( host , port )) == 0 # 0 means connected for port in [ 22 , 80 , 443 , 3000 , 5000 , 8080 ]: print ( port , \"open\" if is_open ( port ) else \"closed\" ) start python3 -m http.server 8080 in another terminal, run the checker, and watch 8080 flip to open."}, {"id": "py-w11-ethics", "title": "Ethics and scope", "src": "Fern", "idea": "Only scan or test systems you own or have written permission to test. Scanning other people's machines can be illegal, even just to look.", "deep": "<ul><li><strong>Your own machine</strong> (127.0.0.1) and your own lab VMs are always fine.</li><li><strong>Practice targets</strong> made for it, like scanme.nmap.org (light scans only) or TryHackMe and Hack The Box labs, are fine within their rules.</li><li><strong>School, work, or public networks</strong>: not without written permission, even with good intentions.</li><li>This is general info, not legal advice. For anything unclear, ask your instructor or the system owner first.</li></ul><p>This connects to your Cybersecurity page when it's built. <span class=\"by\">Added by Fern</span></p>", "text": "your own machine (127.0.0.1) and your own lab vms are always fine. practice targets made for it, like scanme.nmap.org (light scans only) or tryhackme and hack the box labs, are fine within their rules. school, work, or public networks : not without written permission, even with good intentions. this is general info, not legal advice. for anything unclear, ask your instructor or the system owner first. this connects to your cybersecurity page when it's built. added by fern"}, {"id": "py-w11-ex", "title": "Exercise: test your JSON code", "src": "Exercise", "idea": "Write pytest tests for week 8's load_scores and save_scores, using a temporary folder so your real scores file is never touched.", "deep": "<p><strong>Try it:</strong> Test that saving then loading gives back the same dict, and that a missing file loads as <code>{}</code>.</p><details><summary>Hint 1 (nudge)</summary><p>pytest can give each test its own empty temp folder. Look up the <code>tmp_path</code> fixture.</p></details><details><summary>Hint 2 (stronger)</summary><p>Add <code>tmp_path</code> as a test parameter, then pass <code>tmp_path / \"scores.json\"</code> as the path argument.</p></details><details><summary>Answer</summary><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kn\">from</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">scores</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">import</span> <span class=\"n\">load_scores</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">save_scores</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">test_round_trip</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">tmp_path</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">path</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">tmp_path</span> <span class=\"o\">/</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"scores.json\"</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">save_scores</span><span class=\"p\">({</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Noah\"</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"mi\">7</span><span class=\"p\">},</span> <span class=\"n\">path</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">assert</span> <span class=\"n\">load_scores</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">path</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"o\">==</span> <span class=\"p\">{</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Noah\"</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"mi\">7</span><span class=\"p\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">test_missing_file</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">tmp_path</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">assert</span> <span class=\"n\">load_scores</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">tmp_path</span> <span class=\"o\">/</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"nope.json\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"o\">==</span> <span class=\"p\">{}</span></code></pre></div></details><details><summary>🔁 Explain it back: why compare a download's hash with the one on the official site?</summary><p>Think about what it means if even one byte of the file was changed.</p></details>", "text": "try it: test that saving then loading gives back the same dict, and that a missing file loads as {} . hint 1 (nudge) pytest can give each test its own empty temp folder. look up the tmp_path fixture. hint 2 (stronger) add tmp_path as a test parameter, then pass tmp_path / \"scores.json\" as the path argument. answer from scores import load_scores , save_scores def test_round_trip ( tmp_path ): path = tmp_path / \"scores.json\" save_scores ({ \"noah\" : 7 }, path ) assert load_scores ( path ) == { \"noah\" : 7 } def test_missing_file ( tmp_path ): assert load_scores ( tmp_path / \"nope.json\" ) == {} 🔁 explain it back: why compare a download's hash with the one on the official site? think about what it means if even one byte of the file was changed."}]}, {"id": "py-w12", "title": "Week 12 · Mini project", "blurb": "Plan, build and test one small project that uses everything so far. ~4 hrs.", "topics": [{"id": "py-w12-plan", "title": "This week's plan", "src": "Course plan", "idea": "Plan, build and test one small project that uses everything so far.", "deep": "<p>About <strong>3.8 hours</strong> this week, in 6 sessions. Do one per sitting.</p><ol><li><strong>30 min:</strong> Pick and plan (user stories)</li><li><strong>45 min:</strong> Build: core logic</li><li><strong>45 min:</strong> Build: saving to a file</li><li><strong>40 min:</strong> Tests</li><li><strong>35 min:</strong> README and cleanup</li><li><strong>30 min:</strong> Review and what's next</li></ol><p>Reading: <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/whatnow.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tutorial ch. 13: What now?</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/howto/argparse.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">argparse tutorial</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/glossary.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Python Glossary</a>.</p>", "text": "about 3.8 hours this week, in 6 sessions. do one per sitting. 30 min: pick and plan (user stories) 45 min: build: core logic 45 min: build: saving to a file 40 min: tests 35 min: readme and cleanup 30 min: review and what's next reading: tutorial ch. 13: what now? , argparse tutorial , python glossary ."}, {"id": "py-w12-pick", "title": "Pick a project", "src": "Fern", "idea": "Pick one, keep it small, and finish it. A finished small project beats a half-built big one.", "deep": "<ul><li><strong>AAC phrase board (CLI):</strong> boards and phrases saved to JSON, add, remove, search, and \"speak\" with macOS <code>say</code>. Uses weeks 5–9.</li><li><strong>Study timer and log:</strong> 25–45 min sessions, saved to a file, with a weekly summary by subject. Uses weeks 8 and 10.</li><li><strong>Security toolkit:</strong> file hash checker, localhost port checker, and a log-file searcher with regex. Uses weeks 10–11.</li></ul><p>Write 3–5 user stories first, like in CS 307: <em>As a user, I want to … so that ….</em></p>", "text": "aac phrase board (cli): boards and phrases saved to json, add, remove, search, and \"speak\" with macos say . uses weeks 5–9. study timer and log: 25–45 min sessions, saved to a file, with a weekly summary by subject. uses weeks 8 and 10. security toolkit: file hash checker, localhost port checker, and a log-file searcher with regex. uses weeks 10–11. write 3–5 user stories first, like in cs 307: as a user, i want to … so that …."}, {"id": "py-w12-cli", "title": "Make it a real command-line tool", "src": "argparse HOWTO", "idea": "argparse turns command-line arguments into variables and writes --help for you.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">argparse</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">parser</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">argparse</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ArgumentParser</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">description</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"AAC phrase board\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">parser</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">add_argument</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"command\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">choices</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"s2\">\"add\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"list\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"say\"</span><span class=\"p\">])</span>\n<span class=\"n\">parser</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">add_argument</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"text\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">nargs</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"?\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">parser</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">add_argument</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"--board\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">default</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"s2\">\"home\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">args</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">parser</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">parse_args</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"n\">args</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">command</span> <span class=\"o\">==</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"add\"</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"o\">...</span></code></pre></div><div class=\"hl\"><pre><code>python3<span class=\"w\"> </span>aac.py<span class=\"w\"> </span>add<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"I'm hungry\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>--board<span class=\"w\"> </span>home\npython3<span class=\"w\"> </span>aac.py<span class=\"w\"> </span>--help</code></pre></div>", "text": "import argparse parser = argparse . argumentparser ( description = \"aac phrase board\" ) parser . add_argument ( \"command\" , choices = [ \"add\" , \"list\" , \"say\" ]) parser . add_argument ( \"text\" , nargs = \"?\" ) parser . add_argument ( \"--board\" , default = \"home\" ) args = parser . parse_args () if args . command == \"add\" : ... python3 aac.py add \"i'm hungry\" --board home python3 aac.py --help"}, {"id": "py-w12-layout", "title": "Project layout and README", "src": "Fern", "idea": "Give it a folder, a venv, tests, a README, and a Git repo. That's what a portfolio project looks like.", "deep": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code>aac-cli/\n<span class=\"w\">    </span>.venv/<span class=\"w\">              </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">in</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>.gitignore<span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span>aac/\n<span class=\"w\">        </span>__init__.py\n<span class=\"w\">        </span>models.py<span class=\"w\">       </span>Phrase,<span class=\"w\"> </span>Board\n<span class=\"w\">        </span>storage.py<span class=\"w\">      </span>load/save<span class=\"w\"> </span>JSON\n<span class=\"w\">    </span>tests/\n<span class=\"w\">        </span>test_models.py\n<span class=\"w\">    </span>aac.py<span class=\"w\">              </span>argparse<span class=\"w\"> </span>entry<span class=\"w\"> </span>point\n<span class=\"w\">    </span>requirements.txt\n<span class=\"w\">    </span>README.md<span class=\"w\">           </span>what<span class=\"w\"> </span>it<span class=\"w\"> </span>does,<span class=\"w\"> </span>how<span class=\"w\"> </span>to<span class=\"w\"> </span>run<span class=\"w\"> </span>it,<span class=\"w\"> </span>a<span class=\"w\"> </span>screenshot</code></pre></div><p>Push it to GitHub like your study site: <code>git init</code>, commit, create the repo, push.</p>", "text": "aac-cli/ .venv/ ( in .gitignore ) aac/ __init__.py models.py phrase, board storage.py load/save json tests/ test_models.py aac.py argparse entry point requirements.txt readme.md what it does, how to run it, a screenshot push it to github like your study site: git init , commit, create the repo, push."}, {"id": "py-w12-next", "title": "What's next after the course", "src": "Tutorial ch. 13", "idea": "Pick the direction that fits your goals and keep building small things.", "deep": "<ul><li><strong>Security:</strong> Python scripting is a core skill. Next, try <code>scapy</code> and <code>requests</code> in a lab only.</li><li><strong>Web:</strong> Flask or FastAPI connect well with your HTML/CSS and SQL learning.</li><li><strong>Data and SQL:</strong> the built-in <code>sqlite3</code> module lets you practice SQL from Python.</li><li><strong>Practice:</strong> one Exercism or Advent of Code puzzle a few times a week.</li></ul><details><summary>🔁 Explain it back: in your own words, what's the biggest difference between writing Python and writing Java?</summary><p>Pick one thing from week 1 (typing, blocks or running) and explain it with an example from your project.</p></details>", "text": "security: python scripting is a core skill. next, try scapy and requests in a lab only. web: flask or fastapi connect well with your html/css and sql learning. data and sql: the built-in sqlite3 module lets you practice sql from python. practice: one exercism or advent of code puzzle a few times a week. 🔁 explain it back: in your own words, what's the biggest difference between writing python and writing java? pick one thing from week 1 (typing, blocks or running) and explain it with an example from your project."}]}], "ref": [{"id": "py-ref-run", "title": "Run and set up", "html": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code>python3<span class=\"w\">                     </span><span class=\"c1\"># REPL (exit() to leave)</span>\npython3<span class=\"w\"> </span>file.py<span class=\"w\">             </span><span class=\"c1\"># run a script</span>\npython3<span class=\"w\"> </span>-m<span class=\"w\"> </span>venv<span class=\"w\"> </span>.venv<span class=\"w\">       </span><span class=\"c1\"># new venv</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">source</span><span class=\"w\"> </span>.venv/bin/activate<span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"c1\"># use it</span>\npip<span class=\"w\"> </span>install<span class=\"w\"> </span>pkg<span class=\"w\">             </span><span class=\"c1\"># install into venv</span>\npip<span class=\"w\"> </span>freeze<span class=\"w\"> </span>&gt;<span class=\"w\"> </span>requirements.txt\npytest<span class=\"w\"> </span>-v<span class=\"w\">                   </span><span class=\"c1\"># run tests</span></code></pre></div>", "text": "python3 # repl (exit() to leave) python3 file.py # run a script python3 -m venv .venv # new venv source .venv/bin/activate # use it pip install pkg # install into venv pip freeze > requirements.txt pytest -v # run tests"}, {"id": "py-ref-basics", "title": "Basics", "html": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">x</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"mi\">5</span>                  <span class=\"c1\"># no type, no semicolon</span>\n<span class=\"n\">name</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"Noah\"</span>           <span class=\"c1\"># ' or \" both work</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\"> </span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"si\">:</span><span class=\"s2\">.2f</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">age</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"nb\">int</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nb\">input</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Age? \"</span><span class=\"p\">))</span>\n<span class=\"c1\"># comment</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">\"\"\"docstring\"\"\"</span>\n<span class=\"kc\">None</span>  <span class=\"kc\">True</span>  <span class=\"kc\">False</span></code></pre></div>", "text": "x = 5 # no type, no semicolon name = \"noah\" # ' or \" both work print ( f \" { name } { x : .2f } \" ) age = int ( input ( \"age? \" )) # comment \"\"\"docstring\"\"\" none true false"}, {"id": "py-ref-flow", "title": "Control flow", "html": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"n\">x</span> <span class=\"o\">&gt;</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"o\">...</span>\n<span class=\"k\">elif</span> <span class=\"n\">x</span> <span class=\"o\">==</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"o\">...</span>\n<span class=\"k\">else</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"o\">...</span>\n<span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">i</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"nb\">range</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>          <span class=\"c1\"># 0..4</span>\n    <span class=\"o\">...</span>\n<span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">item</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"nb\">enumerate</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">items</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n    <span class=\"o\">...</span>\n<span class=\"k\">while</span> <span class=\"n\">cond</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"o\">...</span>\n<span class=\"k\">match</span> <span class=\"n\">cmd</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">case</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"q\"</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"o\">...</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">case</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">_</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"o\">...</span></code></pre></div>", "text": "if x > 0 : ... elif x == 0 : ... else : ... for i in range ( 5 ): # 0..4 ... for i , item in enumerate ( items ): ... while cond : ... match cmd : case \"q\" : ... case _ : ..."}, {"id": "py-ref-func", "title": "Functions", "html": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">f</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"o\">*</span><span class=\"n\">args</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"o\">**</span><span class=\"n\">kwargs</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"sd\">\"\"\"Docstring.\"\"\"</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"n\">a</span> <span class=\"o\">+</span> <span class=\"n\">b</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>            <span class=\"c1\"># 3</span>\n<span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>       <span class=\"c1\"># keyword argument</span>\n<span class=\"n\">square</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"k\">lambda</span> <span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"n\">x</span> <span class=\"o\">*</span> <span class=\"n\">x</span></code></pre></div>", "text": "def f ( a , b = 2 , * args , ** kwargs ): \"\"\"docstring.\"\"\" return a + b f ( 1 ) # 3 f ( 1 , b = 5 ) # keyword argument square = lambda x : x * x"}, {"id": "py-ref-coll", "title": "Collections", "html": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">nums</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>          <span class=\"c1\"># list</span>\n<span class=\"n\">pt</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>               <span class=\"c1\"># tuple</span>\n<span class=\"n\">ages</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">{</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Ana\"</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"mi\">20</span><span class=\"p\">}</span>        <span class=\"c1\"># dict</span>\n<span class=\"n\">tags</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"p\">{</span><span class=\"s2\">\"py\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"java\"</span><span class=\"p\">}</span>     <span class=\"c1\"># set</span>\n<span class=\"n\">empty_set</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"nb\">set</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>         <span class=\"c1\"># {} is a dict</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">append</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"p\">);</span> <span class=\"n\">nums</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">pop</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>\n<span class=\"n\">ages</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">get</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"Zed\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">v</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">ages</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">items</span><span class=\"p\">():</span> <span class=\"o\">...</span>\n<span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"n\">n</span> <span class=\"o\">*</span> <span class=\"n\">n</span> <span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">n</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">nums</span> <span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"n\">n</span> <span class=\"o\">&gt;</span> <span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>\n<span class=\"p\">{</span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"nb\">len</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">w</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">words</span><span class=\"p\">}</span>\n<span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>  <span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"o\">-</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>  <span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"p\">[::</span><span class=\"o\">-</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">]</span></code></pre></div>", "text": "nums = [ 1 , 2 , 3 ] # list pt = ( 3 , 4 ) # tuple ages = { \"ana\" : 20 } # dict tags = { \"py\" , \"java\" } # set empty_set = set () # {} is a dict nums . append ( 4 ); nums . pop () ages . get ( \"zed\" , 0 ) for k , v in ages . items (): ... [ n * n for n in nums if n > 1 ] { w : len ( w ) for w in words } s [ 1 : 4 ] s [ - 1 ] s [:: - 1 ]"}, {"id": "py-ref-str", "title": "Strings", "html": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">strip</span><span class=\"p\">()</span> <span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">lower</span><span class=\"p\">()</span> <span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">upper</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>\n<span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">split</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\",\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>   <span class=\"s2\">\",\"</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">join</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">parts</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">replace</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"a\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"b\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">startswith</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"x\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>  <span class=\"s2\">\"sub\"</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">s</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">len</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>  <span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>  <span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"o\">-</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>\n<span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"si\">:</span><span class=\"s2\">&gt;6</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span> <span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"si\">:</span><span class=\"s2\">.2f</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span> <span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"si\">:</span><span class=\"s2\">.0%</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span> <span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"si\">:</span><span class=\"s2\">,</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span></code></pre></div>", "text": "s . strip () s . lower () s . upper () s . split ( \",\" ) \",\" . join ( parts ) s . replace ( \"a\" , \"b\" ) s . startswith ( \"x\" ) \"sub\" in s len ( s ) s [ 0 ] s [ - 1 ] f \" { n : >6 } \" f \" { x : .2f } \" f \" { p : .0% } \" f \" { n : , } \""}, {"id": "py-ref-class", "title": "Classes", "html": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"k\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">Dog</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Animal</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"fm\">__init__</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n        <span class=\"nb\">super</span><span class=\"p\">()</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"fm\">__init__</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n        <span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">_secret</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"mi\">1</span>      <span class=\"c1\"># 'private'</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"fm\">__str__</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"bp\">self</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">name</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">from</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">dataclasses</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">import</span> <span class=\"n\">dataclass</span>\n<span class=\"nd\">@dataclass</span>\n<span class=\"k\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">Point</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"nb\">int</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"nb\">int</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span></code></pre></div>", "text": "class dog ( animal ): def __init__ ( self , name ): super () . __init__ ( name ) self . _secret = 1 # 'private' def __str__ ( self ): return self . name from dataclasses import dataclass @dataclass class point : x : int y : int = 0"}, {"id": "py-ref-err", "title": "Files and errors", "html": "<div class=\"hl\"><pre><code><span class=\"k\">with</span> <span class=\"nb\">open</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"f.txt\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"k\">as</span> <span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>      <span class=\"c1\"># \"w\" write, \"a\" append</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">line</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"p\">:</span> <span class=\"o\">...</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nn\">json</span>\n<span class=\"n\">json</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">dump</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">obj</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">indent</span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">obj</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">json</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">load</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">try</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"o\">...</span>\n<span class=\"k\">except</span> <span class=\"ne\">ValueError</span> <span class=\"k\">as</span> <span class=\"n\">e</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"o\">...</span>\n<span class=\"k\">else</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"o\">...</span>\n<span class=\"k\">finally</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"o\">...</span>\n<span class=\"k\">raise</span> <span class=\"ne\">ValueError</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"bad\"</span><span class=\"p\">)</span></code></pre></div>", "text": "with open ( \"f.txt\" ) as f : # \"w\" write, \"a\" append for line in f : ... import json json . dump ( obj , f , indent = 2 ) obj = json . load ( f ) try : ... except valueerror as e : ... else : ... finally : ... raise valueerror ( \"bad\" )"}, {"id": "py-ref-java", "title": "Java → Python", "html": "<table><tr><th>Java</th><th>Python</th></tr><tr><td><code>ArrayList&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><code>list</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>HashMap&lt;K,V&gt;</code></td><td><code>dict</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>HashSet&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><code>set</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>record</code></td><td><code>@dataclass / tuple</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>true / false / null</code></td><td><code>True / False / None</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>&amp;&amp; || !</code></td><td><code>and or not</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>i++</code></td><td><code>i += 1</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>a ? b : c</code></td><td><code>b if a else c</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>for (T x : xs)</code></td><td><code>for x in xs:</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>switch</code></td><td><code>match</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>this</code></td><td><code>self</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>toString() / equals()</code></td><td><code>__str__ / __eq__</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>obj1 == obj2 (same object)</code></td><td><code>obj1 is obj2</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>s1.equals(s2)</code></td><td><code>s1 == s2</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>try / catch / throw</code></td><td><code>try / except / raise</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>try-with-resources</code></td><td><code>with</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>public static void main</code></td><td><code>if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>JUnit @Test</code></td><td><code>def test_...(): assert ...</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Maven / Gradle</code></td><td><code>pip + venv</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>x.size() / x.length()</code></td><td><code>len(x)</code></td></tr></table>", "text": "java python arraylist<t> list hashmap<k,v> dict hashset<t> set record @dataclass / tuple true / false / null true / false / none && || ! and or not i++ i += 1 a ? b : c b if a else c for (t x : xs) for x in xs: switch match this self tostring() / equals() __str__ / __eq__ obj1 == obj2 (same object) obj1 is obj2 s1.equals(s2) s1 == s2 try / catch / throw try / except / raise try-with-resources with public static void main if __name__ == \"__main__\": junit @test def test_...(): assert ... maven / gradle pip + venv x.size() / x.length() len(x)"}], "vocab": [{"title": "Week 1 · Setup", "terms": [["REPL", "Read-Eval-Print Loop: the >>> prompt where Python runs each line as you type it."], ["Interpreter", "The program (python3) that reads and runs Python code directly, with no separate compile step."], ["Dynamic typing", "Types belong to values, not variables, and are checked while the program runs."], ["Indentation", "The spaces at the start of a line. In Python they mark blocks, replacing braces."]]}, {"title": "Week 2 · Basics", "terms": [["f-string", "A string starting with f that fills in {expressions}, like f\"Hi {name}\"."], ["Floor division (//)", "Division that rounds down to a whole number."], ["Slice", "s[start:end:step], a piece of a string or list. The end is excluded."], ["Immutable", "Can't be changed after it's made. Strings and tuples are immutable."], ["None", "Python's \"no value\", like Java's null."]]}, {"title": "Week 3 · Control flow", "terms": [["Truthy / falsy", "How a value acts in an if. Empty things, 0 and None are falsy."], ["range()", "A sequence of numbers for loops: range(start, stop, step), stop excluded."], ["enumerate()", "Gives (index, item) pairs while looping."], ["match", "Python's switch, with pattern matching (3.10+)."]]}, {"title": "Week 4 · Functions", "terms": [["Docstring", "A string right under def that documents the function. help() shows it."], ["Keyword argument", "An argument passed by name, like greet(\"Noah\", punct=\"?\")."], ["*args / **kwargs", "Collect extra positional arguments (tuple) and named arguments (dict)."], ["lambda", "A one-line anonymous function: lambda x: x * 2."]]}, {"title": "Weeks 5–6 · Data structures", "terms": [["List", "An ordered, changeable collection: [1, 2, 3]."], ["Tuple", "An ordered collection that can't change: (3, 4)."], ["Unpacking", "Assigning parts of a sequence to several names: x, y = point."], ["Comprehension", "A one-line loop that builds a list, dict or set."], ["Dict", "Key-value pairs: {\"Ana\": 20}. Like HashMap."], ["Set", "Unique items with no order: {1, 2}. Like HashSet."], ["Hashable", "Can be a dict key or set item because it can't change."]]}, {"title": "Week 7 · Modules", "terms": [["Module", "Any .py file you can import."], ["Package", "A folder of modules, imported with dots."], ["__name__", "A module's name. Equals \"__main__\" when the file is run directly."], ["venv", "A per-project folder holding its own Python and packages."], ["pip / PyPI", "Python's package installer and the online package index it installs from."]]}, {"title": "Week 8 · Files and errors", "terms": [["Context manager", "Something used with with that cleans up after itself, like an open file."], ["JSON", "A text format for data. The json module converts it to and from dicts and lists."], ["Exception", "An error object that stops normal flow until something catches it."], ["Traceback", "The error report Python prints. Read it bottom up."]]}, {"title": "Week 9 · Classes", "terms": [["self", "The current object, written as each method's first parameter. Java's this."], ["__init__", "The method that sets up a new object. Python's constructor."], ["Dunder method", "A __double_underscore__ method that hooks into built-in behavior."], ["Duck typing", "If it has the method you need, you can use it, whatever its class."], ["Dataclass", "A class whose __init__, __repr__ and __eq__ are written for you."], ["Type hint", "An optional label like name: str. Tools check it. Python doesn't enforce it."]]}, {"title": "Weeks 10–12 · Tools", "terms": [["Standard library", "The modules that come with Python, like pathlib, json and re."], ["Regular expression", "A pattern for searching text, used with the re module."], ["pytest", "The most popular Python test runner. Plain assert statements."], ["Fixture", "A pytest helper that sets something up for a test, like tmp_path."], ["Hash", "A fixed-size fingerprint of data. It can't be reversed."], ["Socket", "One end of a network connection."], ["argparse", "The standard module for command-line arguments and --help."]]}], "res": [{"title": "Core sources", "items": [{"title": "The Python Tutorial", "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/", "note": "The official tutorial. The course follows its chapters week by week."}, {"title": "learnpython.org", "url": "https://www.learnpython.org/", "note": "Short interactive lessons that run in the browser. Most weeks point to 1–2 of them for practice."}, {"title": "Python documentation", "url": "https://www.python.org/doc/", "note": "Home for the Library Reference, HOWTOs and the glossary. Use it for lookups."}, {"title": "Python Library Reference", "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/index.html", "note": "Every built-in module, from pathlib to socket. Weeks 10–11 live here."}]}, {"title": "Guides used in the course", "items": [{"title": "Sorting HOWTO", "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/howto/sorting.html", "note": "Week 5: key functions and stable sorts."}, {"title": "Regular Expression HOWTO", "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/howto/regex.html", "note": "Week 10: patterns, groups and raw strings."}, {"title": "Socket Programming HOWTO", "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/howto/sockets.html", "note": "Week 11: how sockets work."}, {"title": "argparse tutorial", "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/howto/argparse.html", "note": "Week 12: command-line tools."}, {"title": "pytest: Get started", "url": "https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/getting-started.html", "note": "Week 11: install and first tests."}]}, {"title": "Optional extras", "items": [{"title": "Python for Everybody (free book)", "url": "https://www.py4e.com/book", "note": "A gentle free textbook. Good when a tutorial chapter feels too dense."}, {"title": "Automate the Boring Stuff (free online)", "url": "https://automatetheboringstuff.com/", "note": "Practical scripts for files, web and spreadsheets. Fits weeks 8–12."}, {"title": "Exercism: Python track", "url": "https://exercism.org/tracks/python", "note": "Free practice problems with optional mentor feedback."}]}], "files": [["12-week Python course outline (Oct 29)", "The course plan you approved. Each week is one unit on the Guide tab."], ["Your three links", "docs.python.org tutorial, python.org docs and learnpython.org, linked from every week's plan card."]]};
